ex_end=&hex[offset of bad char] (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | non-hex char that IS in `ignore`, at an even nibble boundary (state==0), e.g. ignore=\":\" hex=\"ab:cd\" / \":abcd\" / \"abcd:\" | char skipped, ret=0, *bin_len=2 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | char in `ignore` in the MIDDLE of a byte (state==1), e.g. ignore=\":\" hex=\"a:bcd\" | loop breaks -> odd-nibble path: ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | bin_maxlen exhausted while valid hex remains, hex_end!=NULL, e.g. bin_maxlen=1,\"abcd\" | ret=-1, errno=ERANGE, *bin_len=0, *hex_end=&hex[2] (first unconsumed char) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | bin_maxlen exhausted, hex_end==NULL | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL (ERANGE is OVERWRITTEN by the hex_pos!=hex_len branch), *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | bin_maxlen==0 with any valid hex digit | ret=-1; errno=ERANGE if hex_end!=NULL else EINVAL; *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | ERANGE can only trigger at an even nibble boundary (bin_pos check precedes both nibbles but bin_pos is unchanged between them) — so ERANGE and EINVAL(odd) are mutually exclusive | n/a (structural)\nsodium_hex2bin | embedded NUL byte in hex[] with ignore!=NULL (strchr(ignore,0) matches ignore's terminator) | the NUL is treated as an ignorable char and SKIPPED; e.g. bin_maxlen=4, hex=\"ab\\0cd\", hex_len=5, ignore=\":\" -> ret=0, *bin_len=2 (0xab,0xcd), *hex_end=&hex[5] (QUIRK, VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | hex_len==0 | ret=0, *bin_len=0, *hex_end=hex\nsodium_base64_check_variant (called by sodium_base64_encoded_len / sodium_bin2base64 / sodium_base642bin) | ((unsigned)variant & ~0x6U) != 0x1U i.e. variant NOT in {1,3,5,7} — tested 0,2,4,6,8,9,99,-1 all fail | sodium_misuse()/abort (VERIFIED for all of 0,2,4,6,8,9,99,-1)\nsodium_base64_encoded_len | bin_len/3 > (SIZE_MAX-5)/4 | sodium_misuse()/abort (NOTE: the sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN macro instead evaluates to (size_t)SIZE_MAX in that case — divergent behaviour macro vs function)\nsodium_bin2base64 | bin_len/3 > (SIZE_MA