=-1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | '%' present with empty zone (\"fe80::1%\") | ret=-1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | '%' zone present but address has no ':' (IPv4 + zone, \"1.2.3.4%eth0\") | ret=-1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | IPv4 octet > 255 (\"256.0.0.1\"), >3 digits, missing octet (\"1.2.3\"), extra octet (\"1.2.3.4.5\"), trailing junk, empty string \"\", \".\" | ret=-1 (VERIFIED); note leading zeros ARE accepted (\"01.2.3.4\" -> 1.2.3.4)\nsodium_ip2bin | IPv6: >4 hex digits in a group (\"12345::\"), non-hex char (\"g::1\"), single leading ':' not followed by ':' (\":1\"), trailing single ':' (\"1:\"), two '::' (\"2001:db8::1::2\"), >8 groups (\"1:2:3:4:5:6:7:8:9\"), too few groups without '::' (\"2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:0:1\" is 9 groups -> -1), '::' when 16 bytes already filled | ret=-1 (all VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | success | ret=0; IPv4 written as IPv4-mapped: bin[0..9]=0, bin[10]=bin[11]=0xff, bin[12..15]=octets; scan stops at first NUL within ip_len_\nsodium_bin2ip | ip_maxlen <= 2U | returns NULL (VERIFIED)\nsodium_bin2ip | formatted length >= ip_maxlen (needs len+1 bytes) e.g. \"192.168.1.1\" with ip_maxlen=7 | returns NULL (VERIFIED)\nsodium_bin2ip | success | returns `ip`; IPv4-mapped prefix (bin[0..11]==00*10,ff,ff) -> dotted-quad; else lowercase IPv6 with longest zero-run >=2 collapsed to \"::\" (\"1:2:3:4:5:6:1.2.3.4\" round-trips to \"1:2:3:4:5:6:102:304\"; all-zero -> \"::\")\nsodium_memcmp | never fails | 0 if all len bytes equal, -1 otherwise; len==0 -> 0 (VERIFIED); constant-time; __attribute__((warn_unused_result))\nsodium_compare | never fails | -1 if b1<b2, 0 if equal, 1 if b1>b2, comparing LITTLE-ENDIAN (from index len-1 down); len==0 -> 0 (VERIFIED: {ff,00} vs {00,01} -> -1)\nsodium_is_zero | never fails | 1 if all nlen bytes are 0, 0 otherwise; nlen==0 -> 1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_increment | never fails, void | nlen==0 -> no-op; wraps: all-0xff -> all-0x00 (VERIFIED); asm fast paths for nlen==8/12/24 when HAVE_AMD64_ASM\nsodium_add | never fails, void | len==0 -> no-op; wraps mod 2^(8*len) (0xffffffff+1 -> 0) (VERIFIED); asm fast paths len==8/12/24\nsodium_sub | never fails, void | len==0 -> no-op; borrow wraps (0 - 1 -> 0xffffffff) (VERIFIED); asm fast path len==64\nsodium_pad | blocksize == 0 | ret=-1; *padded_buflen_p is NOT written (VERIFIED)\nsodium_pad | (size_t)SIZE_MAX - unpadded_buflen <= xpadlen (arith overflow; e.g. unpadded_buflen=SIZE_MAX-2, blocksize=16) | sodium_misuse()/abort — NOT -1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_pad | xpadded_len >= max_buflen (i.e. max_buflen < *padded_buflen_p) | ret=-1; *padded_buflen_p NOT written (VERIFIED: max_buflen == padded_buflen is OK, padded_buflen-1 fails)\nsodium_pad | success | ret=0; *padded_buflen_p (if non-NULL; NULL is tolerated) = smallest multiple of blocksize STRICTLY greater than unpadded_buflen; writes 0x80 at buf[unpadded_buflen] then 0x00 up to *padded_buflen_p-1 (VERIFIED for blocksize 1,2,16,17,256)\nsodium_unpad | blocksize == 0 | ret=-1, *unpadded_buflen_p NOT written (VERIFIED)\nsodium_unpad | padded_buflen < blocksize | ret=-1, *unpadded_buflen_p NOT written (VERIFIED)\nsodium_unpad | no 0x80 barrier preceded only by 0x00s within the last `blocksize` bytes (all-zero buffer, all-0xff buffer, 0x81 terminator, nonzero byte after the barrier, barrier farther back than blocksize) | ret=-1 AND *unpadded_buflen_p IS written = padded_buflen-1 (garbage-but-defined; unlike the two early returns) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_unpad | unpadded_buflen_p == NULL | dereferenced unconditionally on the non-early-return paths -> crash (no NULL check, unlike sodium_pad)\nsodium_unpad | success | ret=0, *unpadded_buflen_p = padded_buflen-1-pad_len; padded_buflen need NOT be a multiple of blocksize (VERIFIED: pl=17,bs=16 works)\nsodium_memzero | HAVE_MEMSET_S build and memset_s() fails with len>0 | sodium_misuse()/abort; otherwise void, len==0 is a no-op\nsodium_stackzero | len larger than remaining stack (VLA/alloca) | undefined behaviour / stack overflow; no return value\n_sodium_alloc_init | HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC and page_size < CANARY_SIZE(16) or < sizeof(size_t) | sodium_misuse()/abort; otherwise returns 0 always\nsodium_malloc | HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC and size >= SIZE_MAX - page_size*4U | NULL, errno=ENOMEM\nsodium_malloc | underlying mmap/posix_memalign/VirtualAlloc failure | NULL (errno from the allocator)\nsodium_malloc | HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC and page_size <= sizeof(canary)(16) or < sizeof(size_t) | sodium_misuse()/abort\nsodium_malloc | size == 0 | non-NULL pointer to a 0-byte user region (malloc(1) in the non-aligned build) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_malloc | success | non-NULL, region prefilled with GARBAGE_VALUE 0xdb (VERIFIED — header comment saying 0xd0 is stale)\nsodium_allocarray | count > 0 && size >= SIZE_MAX/count (e.g. (SIZE_MAX/2, 4)) | NULL, errno=ENOMEM (VERIFIED)\nsodium_allocarray | count == 0 (any size) or size == 0 | delegates to sodium_malloc(0) -> non-NULL, errno untouched (VERIFIED)\nsodium_free | ptr == NULL | no-op, returns (VERIFIED)\nsodium_free | pointer not from sodium_malloc: canary mismatch (HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC build) | _out_of_bounds() -> raise(SIGPROT/SIGSEGV/SIGKILL) then abort() — uncatchable by a misuse handler\nsodium_free / sodium_mprotect_* | user pointer so low that (canary_ptr & ~page_mask) <= page_size*2 | sodium_misuse()/abort from _unprotected_ptr_from_user_ptr()\nsodium_mprotect_noaccess / _readonly / _readwrite | build WITHOUT HAVE_PAGE_PROTECTION (no mprotect / no aligned malloc) | ret=-1, errno=ENOSYS (VERIFIED in the unconfigured build)\nsodium_mprotect_noaccess / _readonly / _readwrite | HAVE_PAGE_PROTECTION build, ptr not from sodium_malloc | reads a bogus size from the \"base page\" and calls mprotect -> typically -1 with errno=EINVAL/ENOMEM, or misuse/SIGSEGV; success path returns 0\nsodium_mlock | no HAVE_MLOCK and not WINAPI_DESKTOP | ret=-1, errno=ENOSYS; on Linux: mlock() errno ENOMEM (RLIMIT_MEMLOCK exceeded) or EPERM -> -1 (VERIFIED -1 in sandbox)\nsodium_munlock | same as mlock | zeroes the region first (sodium_memzero) then -1/errno as above; returns munlock() result\nsodium_init | first successful call | 0\nsodium_init | any subsequent call (initialized != 0) | 1\nsodium_init | sodium_crit_enter() / sodium_crit_leave() failure (pthread_mutex_lock error, Windows lock init failure) | -1 (no initialization performed)\nsodium_init | any of the sub-initializers calling sodium_misuse (e.g. randombytes_stir cannot open an entropy source) | abort inside sodium_init\nsodium_crit_leave | called while locked==0 | -1, errno=EPERM (where EPERM is defined)\nsodium_misuse | always | calls the handler installed by sodium_set_misuse_handler (outside the lock) if non-NULL, then abort() unconditionally; declared __attribute__((noreturn)) — a handler that returns still aborts\nsodium_set_misuse_handler | handler == NULL | valid: clears the handler; returns 0\nsodium_set_misuse_handler | crit enter/leave failure | -1\nsodium_runtime_has_neon/armcrypto/sse2/sse3/ssse3/sse41/avx/avx2/avx512f/pclmul/aesni/rdrand | never fail | 0 or 1; ALL return 0 before sodium_init() (the static CPUFeatures struct is zero-initialized; _sodium_runtime_get_cpu_features is only called from sodium_init)\nsodium_version_string / sodium_library_version_major / _minor / sodium_library_minimal | never fail | \"1.0.23\" / 30 / 0 / 0 (1 only if compiled with SODIUM_LIBRARY_MINIMAL)\nrandombytes_set_implementation | always | returns 0; never validates the struct. Passing NULL (declared nonnull) is not rejected: implementation becomes NULL and the next call re-initializes to the DEFAULT (&randombytes_sysrandom_implementation) and stirs — i.e. NULL == \"reset to default\"\nrandombytes_set_implementation | impl with a NULL `implementation_name`, `random`, or `buf` (the \"required\" fields) | no check; NULL-pointer call/crash on first use\nrandombytes_implementation_name | never fails | \"sysrandom\" (default), \"internal\" (randombytes_internal_implementation), \"js\" (__EMSCRIPTEN__), or whatever a custom impl returns\nrandombytes_random | never fails | uint32_t from implementation->random(); may abort via sodium_misuse deep inside the entropy source\nrandombytes_uniform | upper_bound == 0 | returns 0 (no error) — unless a custom impl provides `uniform`, which is then called with 0\nrandombytes_uniform | upper_bound == 1 | returns 0\nrandombytes_uniform | upper_bound >= 2 | uniform in [0, upper_bound-1]; min = (1U + ~upper_bound) % upper_bound; rejection-samples randombytes_random() while r < min. upper_bound=0xffffffff -> min=1, result in [0,0xfffffffe]; upper_bound=0x80000001 is the worst case (~2 draws)\nrandombytes_uniform | implementation->uniform != NULL | delegates entirely to impl->uniform(upper_bound), including upper_bound 0/1\nrandombytes_stir | void | calls impl->stir() only if non-NULL; on the internal impl a pid change is NOT checked here but stir resets nonce/pool\nrandombytes_buf | size == 0 | initializes the impl if needed but calls nothing; buf untouched\nrandombytes_buf | sysrandom: getrandom() fails, or fd==-1, or short read; Windows RtlGenRandom fails or size > 0xffffffffUL | sodium_misuse()/abort\nrandombytes_buf_deterministic | SIZE_MAX > 0x4000000000 build and size > 0x4000000000 (2^38) | sodium_misuse()/abort\nrandombytes_buf_deterministic | valid | void; buf = ChaCha20-IETF keystream, key=seed (32 bytes), nonce = the 12 bytes 'L','i','b','s','o','d','i','u','m','D','R','G'; size==0 writes nothing; fully deterministic (differentially testable)\nrandombytes_seedbytes | never fails | 32\nrandombytes_close | implementation == NULL (never used) or impl->close == NULL | 0\nrandombytes_close | sysrandom on Linux with getrandom available | 0 (idempotent); /dev/urandom path: 0 on the first successful close(fd), -1 on subsequent calls (fd already -1)\nrandombytes_close | internal impl: no entropy source flagged available and fd already closed | -1\nrandombytes (NaCl compat) | buf_len > SIZE_MAX (32-bit only) | assert() abort in debug builds; otherwise truncating cast then randombytes_buf\nrandombytes_internal_random (all entry points) | HAVE_GETPID build and getpid() != the pid recorded at stir time (i.e. after fork) | sodium_misuse()/abort\nrandombytes_internal_random_init | no getentropy/getrandom and /dev/urandom+/dev/random both unopenable (errno EIO) | sodium_misuse()/abort\nrandombytes_internal_random_stir | gettimeofday() fails | sodium_misuse()/abort; also misuse if the entropy read for the 32-byte key fails\nrandombytes_sysrandom_init | /dev/urandom and /dev/random both unopenable | sodium_misuse()/abort\ncrypto_ipcrypt_* (encrypt/decrypt, nd_encrypt/nd_decrypt, ndx_encrypt/ndx_decrypt, pfx_encrypt/pfx_decrypt, all four *_keygen) | NO ERROR PATHS EXIST | all return `void`; no length/validation arguments, no -1, no NULL, no sodium_misuse. There are NO string (\"_str\") variants and no int-returning functions in this version's crypto_ipcrypt.h — IP-string parsing errors live only in sodium_ip2bin/sodium_bin2ip (codecs.c). Buffers are fixed-size and unchecked; a short `out` is caller UB\ncrypto_ipcrypt_bytes / keybytes / nd_keybytes / nd_tweakbytes / nd_inputbytes / nd_outputbytes / ndx_keybytes / ndx_tweakbytes / ndx_inputbytes / ndx_outputbytes / pfx_keybytes / pfx_bytes | never fail | 16,16,16,8,16,24,32,16,16,32,32,16\ncrypto_ipcrypt_ndx_encrypt/ndx_decrypt/pfx_encrypt/pfx_decrypt | degenerate key where the two 16-byte halves produce identical round key [ROUNDS/2] (e.g. k[0..15]==k[16..31]) | no error: silently re-derives the second key schedule from k[i]^0x5a and proceeds (deterministic, must be mirrored)\n_crypto_ipcrypt_pick_best_implementation | never fails | returns 0; selects armcrypto (HAVE_ARMCRYPTO+NATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN+sodium_runtime_has_armcrypto), else aesni (HAVE_AVXINTRIN_H+HAVE_WMMINTRIN_H+sodium_runtime_has_aesni), else soft. All three must be bit-identical\n\n=== DELIVERABLE 2 — CONFIG AXES / SHAPES (valid-input axes the code branches on) ===\n\nCONSTANTS | sodium_base64_VARIANT_ORIGINAL=1, sodium_base64_VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING=3, sodium_base64_VARIANT_URLSAFE=5, sodium_base64_VARIANT_URLSAFE_NO_PADDING=7; internal VARIANT_NO_PADDING_MASK=0x2, VARIANT_URLSAFE_MASK=0x4; valid iff (variant & ~0x6)==1\nCONSTANTS | randombytes_SEEDBYTES=32U; randombytes_BYTES_MAX=SODIUM_MIN(SODIUM_SIZE_MAX,0xffffffffUL)=0xffffffff; deterministic size cap=0x4000000000 (2^38); DRG nonce=\"LibsodiumDRG\" (12 bytes, crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_NONCEBYTES=12, KEYBYTES=32)\nCONSTANTS | CANARY_SIZE=16U, GARBAGE_VALUE=0xdb, DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE=0x10000 (or PAGE_SIZE), sodium_malloc layout = [size page RO][guard page][unprotected pages][guard page], user region preceded by a 16-byte canary\nCONSTANTS | crypto_ipcrypt_BYTES=16U, _KEYBYTES=16U, _ND_KEYBYTES=16U, _ND_TWEAKBYTES=8U, _ND_INPUTBYTES=16U, _ND_OUTPUTBYTES=24U, _NDX_KEYBYTES=32U, _NDX_TWEAKBYTES=16U, _NDX_INPUTBYTES=16U, _NDX_OUTPUTBYTES=32U, _PFX_KEYBYTES=32U, _PFX_BYTES=16U; AES ROUNDS=10\nCONSTANTS | SODIUM_VERSION_STRING=\"1.0.23\", SODIUM_LIBRARY_VERSION_MAJOR=30, SODIUM_LIBRARY_VERSION_MINOR=0, sodium_library_minimal()=0\nhex axis | bin_len = 0,1,2,3,16,large; hex_maxlen = exactly bin_len*2+1 (minimum legal) and oversized; output is always lowercase, always NUL-terminated at index bin_len*2\nhex axis | hex2bin input case: lowercase, UPPERCASE, MiXeD (all accepted — \"AbCd\" -> ab cd)\nhex axis | hex2bin ignore = NULL vs \":\" vs \" \\n\" vs \"\" (empty string ignores nothing except the NUL quirk); separators leading / trailing / between bytes (legal) vs mid-byte (illegal)\nhex axis | hex2bin hex_end = NULL vs non-NULL — this flips several failures between error and success; test BOTH for every input\nhex axis | hex2bin bin_maxlen = exact (hex_len/2), oversized, undersized, 0; hex_len = 0, 1, 2, 3, odd, even\nhex axis | round-trip bin2hex -> hex2bin for bin_len 0..64\nbase64 axis | FULL CROSS PRODUCT: variant {1,3,5,7} x bin_len {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,32,33,large} (covers bin_len mod 3 = 0,1,2) x b64_maxlen {exact ENCODED_LEN, ENCODED_LEN+k oversized}\nbase64 axis | sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN / sodium_base64_encoded_len (VERIFIED table, includes trailing NUL): bin_len 0..8 -> variant 1 and 5 (padded): 1,5,5,5,9,9,9,13,13 ; variant 3 and 7 (no padding): 1,3,4,5,7,8,9,11,12 ; bin_len 32 -> 45 (padded) / 44 (nopad) ; bin_len 33 -> 45 (both)\nbase64 axis | encoded alphabets: variants 1/3 use A-Za-z0-9+/ ; variants 5/7 use A-Za-z0-9-_ ; padded variants pad the last group to 4 chars with '='; VERIFIED outputs for bin={ff,01,fe,02,fd,03}: v1 \"\"/\"/w==\"/\"/wE=\"/\"/wH+\"/\"/wH+Ag==\"/\"/wH+Av0=\"/\"/wH+Av0D\" ; v3 \"\"/\"/w\"/\"/wE\"/\"/wH+\"/\"/wH+Ag\"/\"/wH+Av0\"/\"/wH+Av0D\" ; v5 \"\"/\"_w==\"/\"_wE=\"/\"_wH-\"/\"_wH-Ag==\"/\"_wH-Av0=\"/\"_wH-Av0D\" ; v7 \"\"/\"_w\"/\"_wE\"/\"_wH-\"/\"_wH-Ag\"/\"_wH-Av0\"/\"_wH-Av0D\"\nbase64 axis | bin2base64 zero-fills b64[b64_len .. b64_maxlen-1] — compare the WHOLE output buffer, not just the C string\nbase64 axis | base642bin: b64_len mod 4 = 0,2,3 (1 is always invalid); bin_maxlen exact vs oversized vs 0; b64_end NULL vs non-NULL; ignore NULL vs \" \\n\" vs \" \\n\\r\" vs \"=\" (the '=' case is pathological); input with leading/interior/trailing ignorable chars\nbase64 axis | base642bin: 'A' is the only char mapping to value 0 (b64_char_to_byte returns 0xFF for everything else that isn't in the alphabet) — test \"A\", \"AA\", \"AAAA\", \"A===\" per variant\nbase64 axis | round-trip bin2base64 -> base642bin for all 4 variants x bin_len 0..64, with bin_maxlen exact and with b64_end/*bin_len both NULL and non-NULL\nip axis | sodium_ip2bin: IPv4 dotted quad (incl. leading zeros \"01.2.3.4\"), IPv4-mapped (\"::ffff:1.2.3.4\" and \"::ffff:192.168.1.1\" both -> ...ffff+4 octets), full 8-group IPv6, \"::\", \"::1\", collapsed \"2001:db8::1\", 6-group + embedded IPv4 (\"1:2:3:4:5:6:1.2.3.4\"), zone id \"fe80::1%eth0\" (zone parsed and DISCARDED), ip_len_ longer than the NUL-terminated string (stops at NUL), ip_len_ shorter than the string (prefix parse)\nip axis | sodium_bin2ip: ip_maxlen 3..46 (46 = internal buffer size); IPv4-mapped prefix branch vs IPv6 branch; longest-zero-run collapsing (run must be >= 2 to collapse; ties keep the FIRST longest run per the `>` comparison); output is lowercase hex, no leading zeros; round-trip ip2bin -> bin2ip is NOT identity for IPv6 (canonicalizes: \"0:0:0:0:0:0:0:0\" -> \"::\", \"1:2:3:4:5:6:1.2.3.4\" -> \"1:2:3:4:5:6:102:304\")\npad/unpad axis | blocksize = 1,2,16,17,256 (power-of-two path uses `& (blocksize-1)`, non-power-of-two uses `%` — both must agree) x unpadded_buflen = 0,1,blocksize-1,blocksize,blocksize+1,2*blocksize\npad/unpad axis | max_buflen = exactly *padded_buflen_p (accepted) vs *padded_buflen_p-1 (rejected) vs huge; padded_buflen_p = NULL (accepted by pad, NOT by unpad)\npad/unpad axis | VERIFIED padded lengths: ul=0 -> blocksize; ul=blocksize -> 2*blocksize; ul=15,bs=16 -> 16; ul=16,bs=16 -> 32; ul=3,bs=17 -> 17; padding bytes are 0x80 then 0x00...; bytes before unpadded_buflen are preserved\npad/unpad axis | unpad: padded_buflen a multiple of blocksize AND not (pl=17,bs=16 is legal); barrier position 0..blocksize-1 from the end; buffers of all-0x00 and all-0xff are invalid padding; a lone {0x80} with blocksize=1 is valid (unpadded=0)\ncompare axis | sodium_memcmp / sodium_compare / sodium_is_zero with len = 0,1,2,8,16,24,32,64; all-0x00, all-0xff, differing only in byte 0 (LSB, dominates compare), differing only in byte len-1 (MSB), equal buffers, aliased pointers (b1==b2)\nincrement/add/sub axis | len = 0,1,2,8,12,16,24,32,64 (8/12/24 hit the sodium_increment/sodium_add AMD64 asm paths, 64 hits the sodium_sub asm path — MUST be covered to catch asm/portable divergence); carry patterns: all-0xff (wrap to 0), 0x00..0x00, 0xff in low bytes only (partial carry), a+b with carry out of the top byte, 0-1 borrow chain\nmalloc axis | sodium_malloc(size) for size = 0,1,16,4095,4096,page_size-1,page_size,page_size+1,10*page_size; verify prefill 0xdb, then a write/read of the whole region, then sodium_free; sodium_allocarray(count,size) for (0,n),(n,0),(1,1),(1024,16),(SIZE_MAX/2,4) overflow\nmprotect lifecycle axis | sodium_malloc -> write -> sodium_mprotect_readonly (0) -> read -> sodium_mprotect_noaccess (0) -> sodium_mprotect_readwrite (0) -> write -> sodium_free; also mprotect_* immediately after malloc and repeated/idempotent calls. Config gate: HAVE_PAGE_PROTECTION = HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC && (WINAPI_DESKTOP || HAVE_MPROTECT); without it every sodium_mprotect_* is -1/ENOSYS and sodium_malloc is plain malloc with no canary/guard pages (the Rust port must pick one behaviour and document it)\nrandombytes injection axis | struct randombytes_implementation (randombytes.h) — EXACT field order and signatures: [1] const char *(*implementation_name)(void) REQUIRED; [2] uint32_t (*random)(void) REQUIRED; [3] void (*stir)(void) OPTIONAL/NULLable; [4] uint32_t (*uniform)(const uint32_t upper_bound) OPTIONAL/NULLable (default rejection sampler used when NULL); [5] void (*buf)(void *const buf, const size_t size) REQUIRED; [6] int (*close)(void) OPTIONAL/NULLable. randombytes_set_implementation(&impl) returns 0 and DOES let a test inject a fully deterministic RNG — so randombytes_random / randombytes_uniform / randombytes_buf / crypto_ipcrypt_*_keygen / any keygen become differentially testable byte-for-byte. Set `uniform=NULL` to exercise libsodium's own rejection sampler over a counter-based `random`, and set `uniform=<fn>` to test the delegation path\nrandombytes axis | randombytes_buf_deterministic(buf, size, seed) with fixed seeds (all-0x00, all-0xff, 0x00..0x1f, ASCII) x size = 0,1,31,32,33,64,1000,65536 — fully deterministic ChaCha20-IETF keystream, the primary C-vs-Rust comparison point\nrandombytes axis | randombytes_uniform bounds only (unless a deterministic impl is injected): 0 -> 0, 1 -> 0, 2, 3, 7, 0x7fffffff, 0x80000001 (worst case ~2 draws), 0xffffffff (min=1, result <= 0xfffffffe)\nrandombytes axis | implementation selection: default &randombytes_sysrandom_implementation (\"sysrandom\"), &randombytes_internal_implementation (\"internal\", uniform=NULL, close defined), custom, and NULL (resets to default); randombytes_seedbytes()==32; randombytes_stir()/randombytes_close() idempotency; init-on-first-use (any randombytes_* call auto-inits and stirs)\nsodium_init axis | first call -> 0, every later call -> 1; all sodium_runtime_has_*() return 0 before init; sodium_set_misuse_handler before/after init; handler NULL vs non-NULL (a handler is the only way to observe the ~20 sodium_misuse sites without dying)\ncrypto_ipcrypt axis | 4 modes: deterministic (16B in/16B out, key 16), nd (in 16, tweak 8, out 24 = tweak||ct; decrypt takes the 24B blob), ndx (in 16, tweak 16, out 32 = tweak||ct, key 32 = two 16B halves), pfx (in 16, out 16, key 32, prefix-preserving)\ncrypto_ipcrypt axis | input shape: IPv4-mapped 16-byte form (bin[0..9]=0,bin[10..11]=0xff — pfx detects this and starts at prefix bit 96, forcing out[10..11]=0xff so IPv4 stays IPv4) vs native IPv6 (pfx starts at bit 0); all-zero IP, all-0xff IP; feed via sodium_ip2bin(\"192.0.2.1\") and sodium_ip2bin(\"2001:db8::1\")\ncrypto_ipcrypt axis | key shapes: all-zero, all-0xff, distinct halves, IDENTICAL halves (triggers the k[i]^0x5a re-derivation in ndx_encrypt/ndx_decrypt/pfx_encrypt/pfx_decrypt); tweak shapes: all-zero, all-0xff, counter\ncrypto_ipcrypt axis | round-trips: encrypt/decrypt, nd_encrypt/nd_decrypt (nd_decrypt reads the tweak from in[0..7]), ndx_encrypt/ndx_decrypt (tweak from in[0..15]), pfx_encrypt/pfx_decrypt; plus the pfx prefix-preservation property (two IPs sharing an n-bit prefix produce ciphertexts sharing an n-bit prefix); implementations soft/aesni/armcrypto must be identical"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a8681fd7ddf4edf24 (use SendMessage with to: 'a8681fd7ddf4edf24', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 160264\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 544983</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"7e5a2d94-c867-4954-8872-a00fd324afd7","timestamp":"2026-08-14T16:34:05.851Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are analyzing the ORIGINAL C source of libsodium 1.0.23 to mechanically enumerate its ERROR SURFACE and CONFIGURATION SURFACE. Do NOT modify any file. Only READ and REPORT.\n\nRoot: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust/c_src/libsodium\n\nYOUR AREA (only these dirs/files):\n- sodium/codecs.c      (hex + base64 encode/decode)\n- sodium/utils.c       (sodium_memcmp, compare, is_zero, increment, add, sub, pad, unpad, malloc/allocarray/free/mprotect_*, memzero, stackzero, bin2hex/hex2bin live in codecs.c)\n- sodium/core.c        (sodium_init, sodium_set_misuse_handler, sodium_misuse)\n- sodium/runtime.c     (sodium_runtime_has_*)\n- sodium/version.c\n- randombytes/         (randombytes.c, internal/randombytes_internal_random.c, sysrandom/randombytes_sysrandom.c)\n- crypto_ipcrypt/      (crypto_ipcrypt.c, ipcrypt_soft.c)\n\nUseful starting greps (run them, then READ the surrounding code carefully):\n  grep -rn \"--include=*.c\" -E \"return -1|return NULL|abort\\(\\)|sodium_misuse|assert\\(|errno|EINVAL|ENOMEM|SIZE_MAX|== NULL\" <dir>\n  Read public headers ../include/sodium/utils.h, core.h, randombytes.h, crypto_ipcrypt.h, version.h and record every constant/macro + numeric value (esp. sodium_base64_VARIANT_* values, crypto_ipcrypt_* sizes).\n\nDELIVERABLE 1 — ERROR ROWS. One row per DISTINCT rejection/error branch. Format as a single pipe-delimited line:\n  function_name | exact trigger condition (the precise invalid input) | expected C result (exact return value: -1, NULL, abort, and what *_len / errno is set to)\nBe EXHAUSTIVE. Specifically cover:\n- sodium_bin2hex: hex_maxlen <= bin_len*2U -> sodium_misuse()/abort.\n- sodium_hex2bin: every branch — odd nibble, non-hex char with ignore==NULL -> -1 + errno EINVAL, char in `ignore` set, bin_maxlen exceeded -> -1 + errno ERANGE, hex_end handling when hex_end==NULL and leftover -> -1 EINVAL, bin_len output.\n- sodium_bin2base64: b64_maxlen < needed -> sodium_misuse(); each variant's padding behaviour.\n- sodium_base642bin: EVERY branch — invalid char -> -1 EINVAL, bin_maxlen overflow -> -1 ERANGE, wrong padding for VARIANT_*_NO_PADDING vs padded, trailing bits nonzero, `=` handling, b64_end NULL with leftover -> -1 EINVAL, and each of the 4 variants: sodium_base64_VARIANT_ORIGINAL(1), ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING(3), URLSAFE(5), URLSAFE_NO_PADDING(7). ALSO: what does an INVALID variant value (e.g. 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 99) do? Read b64_byte_to_char/b64_char_to_byte and the variant checks — is there an abort/sodium_misuse for an unknown variant? Report exactly.\n- sodium_base64_encoded_len / ENCODED_LEN macro for each variant.\n- sodium_pad: blocksize 0 -> ? , SIZE_MAX overflow -> -1, xpadded_len >= max_padded_len -> -1.\n- sodium_unpad: blocksize 0, padded_len < blocksize -> -1, invalid padding -> -1.\n- sodium_malloc/allocarray: size overflow (count*size) -> NULL + errno ENOMEM, size 0.\n- sodium_mprotect_noaccess/readonly/readwrite on non-sodium_malloc ptr.\n- sodium_compare / memcmp / is_zero / increment / add / sub: any length-0 behaviour, return values (-1/0/1 for compare).\n- sodium_init: repeated calls return 1, concurrent/failed -> -1.\n- sodium_set_misuse_handler(NULL).\n- randombytes_set_implementation, randombytes_uniform(0) / upper_bound bounds, randombytes_buf_deterministic seed size, randombytes_random, randombytes_stir, randombytes_close return values, randombytes_implementation_name.\n- crypto_ipcrypt_*: every -1 (e.g. bad ip string parse in ipcrypt_pfx / str variants), sizes.\n\nDELIVERABLE 2 — CONFIG AXES / SHAPES. Enumerate VALID-input axes the code branches on:\n- hex: bin_len 0,1,2,3,large; hex2bin with ignore=NULL vs \":\" vs \" \\n\"; hex_end non-NULL.\n- base64: FULL cross-product of the 4 variants x input length mod 3 (0,1,2) x bin_len 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,large; base642bin with b64_end, ignore chars, exact-size vs oversized bin_maxlen; round-trip bin2base64->base642bin for all 4 variants.\n- pad/unpad: blocksize 1,2,16,17,256; unpadded_buflen 0,1,blocksize-1,blocksize,blocksize+1.\n- sodium_compare/increment/add/sub: len 1,2,8,16,32 with carry/borrow propagation (all 0xff, all 0x00).\n- sodium_malloc / allocarray / mprotect lifecycle.\n- randombytes_buf_deterministic with fixed seeds (deterministic => comparable between C and Rust!). randombytes_uniform with various upper_bound (1, 2, 3, 0x7fffffff, 0xffffffff) — note it consumes randomness so only bounds/behaviour are comparable, EXCEPT via a custom randombytes_implementation. Report whether randombytes_set_implementation lets a test inject a deterministic RNG (list the struct randombytes_implementation fields and their exact order/signatures from randombytes.h) — this is important for making random-dependent functions differentially testable.\n- crypto_ipcrypt: deterministic/nd/ndx/pfx variants, ipv4 vs ipv6 inputs, sizes.\nName each CONSTANT with its numeric value.\n\nOutput ONLY the two deliverables as plain text lines. Do not write any files.","agentId":"a8681fd7ddf4edf24","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Analysis complete. Below are the two deliverables (verified by compiling `sodium/codecs.c` + `sodium/utils.c` into a standalone probe harness in `/tmp/lsprobe` with a `sodium_misuse()` stub — nothing in the repo was modified).\n\nFiles read: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust/c_src/libsodium/sodium/{codecs.c,utils.c,core.c,runtime.c,version.c}`, `/…/randombytes/{randombytes.c,internal/randombytes_internal_random.c,sysrandom/randombytes_sysrandom.c}`, `/…/crypto_ipcrypt/{crypto_ipcrypt.c,ipcrypt_soft.c,implementations.h}`, `/…/include/sodium/{utils.h,core.h,randombytes.h,crypto_ipcrypt.h,version.h,export.h,private/common.h}`.\n\n=== DELIVERABLE 1 — ERROR ROWS (function | trigger | expected C result) ===\n\nsodium_bin2hex | bin_len >= SIZE_MAX/2 | sodium_misuse() -> misuse handler (if set via sodium_set_misuse_handler) then abort(); never returns\nsodium_bin2hex | hex_maxlen <= bin_len*2U (needs bin_len*2+1; e.g. bin_len=0/hex_maxlen=0, bin_len=1/hex_maxlen<=2, bin_len=3/hex_maxlen<=6) | sodium_misuse()/abort (VERIFIED)\nsodium_bin2hex | valid call | returns `hex` (same pointer); writes exactly bin_len*2 lowercase hex chars + 1 NUL; does NOT touch bytes past index bin_len*2\nsodium_hex2bin | odd number of hex digits consumed (state!=0 at loop end), e.g. hex=\"abc\" | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len=0, *hex_end=&hex[hex_pos-1] (points AT the last consumed digit, i.e. offset 2 for \"abc\") (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | non-hex char, ignore==NULL, hex_end==NULL, e.g. bin_maxlen=4,\"ab:cd\" | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len = number of bytes decoded BEFORE the bad char (1, i.e. NOT reset to 0 — the bin_pos=0 reset happens before this branch) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | non-hex char, ignore==NULL, hex_end!=NULL | ret=0 (NOT an error), errno untouched, *bin_len=decoded prefix len, *hex_end=&hex[offset of bad char] (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | non-hex char that IS in `ignore`, at an even nibble boundary (state==0), e.g. ignore=\":\" hex=\"ab:cd\" / \":abcd\" / \"abcd:\" | char skipped, ret=0, *bin_len=2 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | char in `ignore` in the MIDDLE of a byte (state==1), e.g. ignore=\":\" hex=\"a:bcd\" | loop breaks -> odd-nibble path: ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | bin_maxlen exhausted while valid hex remains, hex_end!=NULL, e.g. bin_maxlen=1,\"abcd\" | ret=-1, errno=ERANGE, *bin_len=0, *hex_end=&hex[2] (first unconsumed char) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | bin_maxlen exhausted, hex_end==NULL | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL (ERANGE is OVERWRITTEN by the hex_pos!=hex_len branch), *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | bin_maxlen==0 with any valid hex digit | ret=-1; errno=ERANGE if hex_end!=NULL else EINVAL; *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | ERANGE can only trigger at an even nibble boundary (bin_pos check precedes both nibbles but bin_pos is unchanged between them) — so ERANGE and EINVAL(odd) are mutually exclusive | n/a (structural)\nsodium_hex2bin | embedded NUL byte in hex[] with ignore!=NULL (strchr(ignore,0) matches ignore's terminator) | the NUL is treated as an ignorable char and SKIPPED; e.g. bin_maxlen=4, hex=\"ab\\0cd\", hex_len=5, ignore=\":\" -> ret=0, *bin_len=2 (0xab,0xcd), *hex_end=&hex[5] (QUIRK, VERIFIED)\nsodium_hex2bin | hex_len==0 | ret=0, *bin_len=0, *hex_end=hex\nsodium_base64_check_variant (called by sodium_base64_encoded_len / sodium_bin2base64 / sodium_base642bin) | ((unsigned)variant & ~0x6U) != 0x1U i.e. variant NOT in {1,3,5,7} — tested 0,2,4,6,8,9,99,-1 all fail | sodium_misuse()/abort (VERIFIED for all of 0,2,4,6,8,9,99,-1)\nsodium_base64_encoded_len | bin_len/3 > (SIZE_MAX-5)/4 | sodium_misuse()/abort (NOTE: the sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN macro instead evaluates to (size_t)SIZE_MAX in that case — divergent behaviour macro vs function)\nsodium_bin2base64 | bin_len/3 > (SIZE_MAX-5)/4 | sodium_misuse()/abort\nsodium_bin2base64 | b64_maxlen <= required b64_len (i.e. b64_maxlen < sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN(bin_len,variant)) | sodium_misuse()/abort (VERIFIED for all 4 variants, bin_len 0..6, at need-1)\nsodium_bin2base64 | valid call | returns `b64`; writes b64_len chars then ZERO-FILLS the whole remainder: `do { b64[b64_pos++]=0; } while (b64_pos < b64_maxlen)` — exactly b64_maxlen bytes are written, not just b64_len+1\nsodium_base642bin | invalid b64 char (d==0xFF) not in `ignore`, b64_end==NULL, and it is not at b64_len | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len may be NONZERO (partial decode retained when the failure comes from the b64_end==NULL branch): v=3,\"/w*=\",bin_maxlen=8 -> ret=-1,EINVAL,*bin_len=1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | invalid char reached in a PADDED variant where padding was expected (skip_padding sees non-'=' non-ignored char) | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len=0: v=1,\"/w*=\" -> ret=-1,EINVAL,*bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | invalid char with b64_end!=NULL and no padding obligation | ret=0 (NOT an error), *b64_end=&b64[pos of bad char], *bin_len=decoded prefix (VERIFIED: v=3,\"/w*=\" -> ret=0,*bin_len=1,*b64_end=b64+2)\nsodium_base642bin | one leftover b64 char (chars consumed % 4 == 1 => acc_len==6 > 4), e.g. \"A\" or \"QUJDQ\" | ret=-1, errno NOT SET/unchanged (no errno assignment on this branch!), *bin_len=0, *b64_end=&b64[b64_pos] (VERIFIED, errno stayed 0)\nsodium_base642bin | nonzero trailing bits, e.g. \"AC\" (acc&((1<<acc_len)-1) != 0) | ret=-1, errno NOT SET/unchanged, *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | bin_maxlen exceeded, b64_end!=NULL | ret=-1, errno=ERANGE, *bin_len=0, *b64_end at the char that would have produced the extra byte (bin_maxlen=1,\"/wH+\" -> *b64_end=b64+2) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | bin_maxlen exceeded, b64_end==NULL | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL (ERANGE overwritten by the b64_pos!=b64_len branch), *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | bin_maxlen==0 with any decodable data | ret=-1, ERANGE (or EINVAL if b64_end==NULL), *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | PADDED variant (1 or 5), padding absent/truncated: \"/w\" (needs \"==\") or \"/wE\" (needs \"=\") | ret=-1, errno=ERANGE (from _sodium_base642bin_skip_padding hitting b64_pos>=b64_len — NOT EINVAL), *bin_len=0 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | NO_PADDING variant (3 or 7) fed padded input \"/w==\" or \"/wE=\", b64_end==NULL | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL, *bin_len = partial decode (1 resp. 2 — NONZERO) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | NO_PADDING variant fed padded input, b64_end!=NULL | ret=0, *bin_len=1, *b64_end points AT the first '=' (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | URLSAFE variant (5,7) fed '+' or '/' | those are invalid chars -> break; then either EINVAL (b64_end==NULL) or ret=0 with *b64_end at that char; \"/w\" v=5 -> ret=-1 EINVAL (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | ORIGINAL variant (1,3) fed '-' or '_' | invalid chars, same as above; \"_w\" v=1 -> ret=-1 ERANGE (breaks at pos 0, then missing padding) (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | `ignore` set CONTAINS '=' with a padded variant, e.g. ignore=\"=\", \"/w==\" v=1 | '=' chars are consumed by the main loop's ignore path, so skip_padding runs off the end: ret=-1, errno=ERANGE, *bin_len=0, *b64_end=b64+4 (QUIRK, VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | extra '=' beyond required padding, b64_end==NULL, e.g. \"/w===\" v=1 | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL (leftover byte); with b64_end!=NULL -> ret=0, *bin_len=1, *b64_end=b64+4 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | b64_end==NULL and any unconsumed trailing byte (incl. a trailing NUL when b64_len includes it and ignore==NULL) | ret=-1, errno=EINVAL\nsodium_base642bin | b64_len==0 | ret=0, *bin_len=0, *b64_end=b64 (all variants, VERIFIED)\nsodium_base642bin | on success with ignore!=NULL, trailing ignorable chars are consumed and included in *b64_end | ret=0 (VERIFIED: \"/w =\\n=\" ignore=\" \\n\" v=1 -> *bin_len=1, *b64_end=b64+6)\nsodium_ip2bin | '%' zone present and any zone char is not [0-9a-zA-Z._-] (\"fe80::1%bad!\") | ret=-1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | '%' present with empty zone (\"fe80::1%\") | ret=-1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | '%' zone present but address has no ':' (IPv4 + zone, \"1.2.3.4%eth0\") | ret=-1 (VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | IPv4 octet > 255 (\"256.0.0.1\"), >3 digits, missing octet (\"1.2.3\"), extra octet (\"1.2.3.4.5\"), trailing junk, empty string \"\", \".\" | ret=-1 (VERIFIED); note leading zeros ARE accepted (\"01.2.3.4\" -> 1.2.3.4)\nsodium_ip2bin | IPv6: >4 hex digits in a group (\"12345::\"), non-hex char (\"g::1\"), single leading ':' not followed by ':' (\":1\"), trailing single ':' (\"1:\"), two '::' (\"2001:db8::1::2\"), >8 groups (\"1:2:3:4:5:6:7:8:9\"), too few groups without '::' (\"2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:0:1\" is 9 groups -> -1), '::' when 16 bytes already filled | ret=-1 (all VERIFIED)\nsodium_ip2bin | success | ret=0; IPv4 written as IPv4-mapped: bin[0..9]