was needed; `t00_smoke` still passes.\n\n## (d) Rows not covered / caveats\nNothing is skipped, but two rows required an indirect observation because their thresholds are not allocatable:\n\n- **Rows 245/246/247/252** say \"misuse\", but `SODIUM_SIZE_MAX = min(UINT64_MAX, SIZE_MAX) = UINT64_MAX` on this target and the argument is `unsigned long long`, so `len > MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` is **provably dead code** in the C. The tests assert the bound really is `UINT64_MAX` in both libs and then, in forked children, that `len = 2⁶⁴-1` does **not** produce `SIGABRT` in either lib.\n- **Row 250** is reached *without* allocating 256 GiB: the ic guard is evaluated **before** any memory is touched, so a forked child with a SIGSEGV/SIGBUS handler that `_exit(42)`s cleanly separates \"guard fired\" (`SIGABRT`) from \"guard did not fire, memory access happened\" (`Returned(42)`). This pins the exact arithmetic triple: `(mlen = 64·2³², ic = 0)` → legal, `(64·2³², ic = 1)` → misuse (limit is exactly 0), `(64·2³²+1, ic = 2³²-1)` → **no** misuse (underflow quirk), plus the `mlen+63` wrap cases. The 256 GiB buffer is never allocated.\n\nOne environmental note: forked children now `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, 0)` — without it, `systemd-coredump` dumped every aborting/faulting child and the suite took 100 s instead of 3 s."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aaa58f193a62caee6 (use SendMessage with to: 'aaa58f193a62caee6', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 173335\ntool_uses: 52\nduration_ms: 1326859</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"c27586e6-b88c-4295-a036-43eb1ae65873","timestamp":"2026-08-14T17:08:59.780Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification of libsodium 1.0.23.\n\nREPO: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nYOUR FILE (create it, nothing else): tests/t04_stream.rs\n\n## HARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify: `c_src/**` (the C is ground truth), `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`, or any other `tests/t*.rs`.\n- You MAY read anything. You MAY edit files under `src/` ONLY to fix a genuine Rust translation bug proven by a failing differential test.\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly — every call goes through `dlsym` on BOTH shared libraries via the harness.\n- NEVER weaken/skip an assertion. The C is ALWAYS correct.\n\n## THE HARNESS (tests/common/mod.rs — read it first)\n```rust\nmod common; use common::*;\ninit_both();\nlet (c, r) = fnpair!(\"symbol_name\", unsafe extern \"C\" fn(A,B)->R);\nassert_eq_bytes(\"what\", &c_out, &r_out);\nRng::new(SEED)   // .bytes(n) .byte() .next_u32() .next_u64() .below(n) .fill(&mut b) .pick(&xs)\npub const LENS: &[usize]  // ready-made length sweep with all block boundaries\npub fn patterns(n, &mut rng) -> Vec<Vec<u8>>  // all-0x00, all-0xff, counter, 2 random\nforked(|| -> i64 { ... }) -> Outcome; Outcome::Signaled(SIGABRT); assert_same_fatal(..)\ninstall_det_rng(bool); reset_det_rng();\nlibs(); sym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")   // single-lib lookup; use inside forked closures\n```\n\n## WHAT TO COVER\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows **83–97** and `ERRORS.md` rows **245–257** — they are your specification. Every stream cipher and EVERY entry point, including the low-level `_ic` and `_ext` ones:\n\n- `crypto_stream_chacha20`, `_chacha20_xor`, `_chacha20_xor_ic` (8-byte nonce, **u64** ic)\n- `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf`, `_ietf_xor`, `_ietf_xor_ic` (12-byte nonce, **u32** ic)\n- `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_ext`, `_ietf_ext_xor_ic` (extended counter — LOW-LEVEL, do not skip)\n- `crypto_stream_salsa20`, `_xor`, `_xor_ic` (8-byte nonce, u64 ic)\n- `crypto_stream_salsa2012`, `_xor` (12 rounds; **no** `_xor_ic` exists)\n- `crypto_stream_salsa208`, `_xor` (8 rounds; **no** `_xor_ic`)\n- `crypto_stream_xsalsa20`, `_xor`, `_xor_ic` (24-byte nonce)\n- `crypto_stream_xchacha20`, `_xor`, `_xor_ic` (24-byte nonce)\n- `crypto_stream`, `_xor` (dispatch → xsalsa20) + every `*_keybytes`/`_noncebytes`/`_messagebytes_max`/`_primitive`/`*_keygen`\n- the `_crypto_stream_chacha20_pick_best_implementation` / `_salsa20_...` internal symbols\n\nC sources: `c_src/libsodium/crypto_stream/**`. Headers: `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/crypto_stream*.h`. READ THEM for exact signatures.\n\nCritical shapes (from CONFIGS rows 83–97):\n- length sweep {0,1,31,32,63,**64**,65,127,128,129,191,192,255,256,512,513,1024,4096} — `len==0` must be an early return that writes NOTHING (prefill with 0xAA and assert unchanged in both libs).\n- chacha20 `_xor_ic`: ic ∈ {0, 1, **2^32-1**, **2^32**, 2^32+1, u64::MAX} — this exercises the `j12` wrap into `j13`. Getting this wrong is the classic bug.\n- chacha20_ietf `_xor_ic`: ic ∈ {0, 1, 2^32-2, 2^32-1} plus the guard boundary `ic == 2^32 - ceil(mlen/64)` (last legal value, must SUCCEED) and `ic == 2^32 - ceil(mlen/64) + 1` (must `sodium_misuse()` → SIGABRT in BOTH libs — use `forked`).\n- salsa20 `_xor_ic`: ic ∈ {0,1,2^32-1,2^32,u64::MAX-1,u64::MAX} — manual 8-byte little-endian carry chain, no wrap detection.\n- ERRORS row 250 is a QUIRK: for `mlen > 64*2^32` the ietf ic guard underflows and NEVER fires. Do not try to allocate 256 GiB — instead reason about which `(ic, mlen)` pairs are reachable and cover the guard boundary exactly at small `mlen`. Document row 250 as \"not allocatable\" if you cannot reach it without a huge buffer, and say so explicitly in your report.\n- keystream functions (`crypto_stream_chacha20`, `crypto_stream_salsa20`, ...) memset then encrypt; `_xor` variants XOR. Test in-place (`c == m`) and disjoint buffers.\n- key/nonce sweep: use `patterns(32, &mut rng)` × `patterns(nonce_len, &mut rng)`.\n- salsa20/2012/208 have **NO** length bounds check at all (ERRORS 253–254) — assert both libs return 0 for large-but-allocatable lengths.\n\n### Requirements\n1. **Every** CONFIGS row 83–97 gets a `#[test]` (or clearly-named section) with ≥64 randomized inputs.\n2. **Every** ERRORS row 245–257 gets a C-vs-Rust assertion (return value AND output bytes; abort rows via `forked` + `assert_same_fatal`).\n3. Consistency property inside each lib: `xor(m)` must equal `m XOR keystream()`; `xor_ic(ic=0)` must equal `xor()`. Assert in BOTH libs, then compare across libs.\n4. Chunked-vs-single property: encrypting a buffer in two calls with the right `ic` offset must equal one call — this is exactly how the AEADs drive these functions.\n5. Prefill all output buffers with 0xAA and compare the FULL buffer.\n\n## ON DIVERGENCE\n1. Print exact inputs (key, nonce, ic, len), C output, Rust output, first differing byte offset.\n2. Rust sources: `src/primitives/{chacha20,salsa20,salsa_variants,core_salsa,xstream}.rs`.\n3. Fix the Rust to match the C EXACTLY. Usual causes: counter-wrap handling, little-endian counter packing, the `ic` guard arithmetic, the <64-byte tail buffer.\n4. Re-run and confirm.\n5. Report EVERY `src/` edit with a one-line before/after and the proving row.\n\n## BUILD / RUN\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test t04_stream 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n\n## DELIVERABLE\n`tests/t04_stream.rs` compiling and PASSING. Report: (a) test count, (b) CONFIGS/ERRORS rows → test fn mapping, (c) every `src/` fix with the proving row, (d) any row not covered and precisely why.","agentId":"aaa58f193a62caee6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `tests/t04_stream.rs` compiles clean (no warnings) and passes: **34 tests, 0 failures, ~3 s** (`cargo build && cargo test --test t04_stream`; also verified with `--test-threads=1` and across 4 repeat runs).\n\n## (a) Test count\n34 `#[test]` functions, ~1800 lines. Every CONFIGS row test carries a self-checking `assert!(iters >= 64)`. Every output buffer is prefilled with `0xAA` plus a 32-byte trailing guard; the **full** buffer is compared C-vs-Rust and the guard is asserted intact in both libs (this is also the `len == 0` \"writes nothing\" check).\n\n## (b) Row → test mapping\n\n| CONFIGS | test(s) |\n|---|---|\n| 83 | `r83_chacha20_keystream` (245 inputs; keystream ≡ `xor(zeros)`) |\n| 84 | `r84_chacha20_xor` (in-place + disjoint, `xor(m) == m^ks`) |\n| 85 | `r85_chacha20_xor_ic` (ic ∈ {0,1,2³²-1,2³²,2³²+1,2⁶⁴-1}), `r85_chacha20_j12_j13_carry` |\n| 86 | `r86_chacha20_ietf_keystream` |\n| 87 | `r87_chacha20_ietf_xor` |\n| 88 | `r88_chacha20_ietf_xor_ic`, `r88_ietf_xor_ic_last_legal_ic` (`ic == 2³²-ceil(mlen/64)` for every length) |\n| 89 | `r89_chacha20_ietf_ext`, `r89_ietf_ext_xor_ic`, `r89_ietf_ext_counter_overflows_into_iv` |\n| 90 | `r90_salsa20` |\n| 91 | `r91_salsa20_xor_ic` (ic ∈ {0,1,2³²-1,2³²,2⁶⁴-2,2⁶⁴-1}), `r91_salsa20_carry_chain` (carry out of every one of the 8 counter bytes + 2⁶⁴ wrap) |\n| 92 / 93 | `r92_salsa2012` / `r93_salsa208` (+ assert `*_xor_ic` does **not** exist in either lib) |\n| 94 / 95 | `r94_xsalsa20` / `r95_xchacha20` (HSalsa20/HChacha20 subkey delegation + ic pass-through) |\n| 96 | `r96_crypto_stream_dispatch`, `r83_97_constants` (all `_keybytes`/`_noncebytes`/`_messagebytes_max`/`_primitive`), `r83_97_keygen` (all 8 `*_keygen` via `install_det_rng`) |\n| 97 | `r97_key_nonce_pattern_matrix` (5 keys × 5 nonces × 16 lengths × every entry point) |\n| req 3/4 | `family_consistency` (inside every row test) and `chunked_vs_single_all_entry_points` |\n| internals | `internal_pick_best_implementation` (both `_pick_best_implementation` + both `*_ref_implementation` data symbols) |\n\n| ERRORS | test |\n|---|---|\n| 245/246/247/252 | `e245_247_252_size_max_guards_unreachable` |\n| 248 | `e248_ietf_clen_bound` (`64·2³²` passes, `+1` and `2⁶⁴-1` abort) |\n| 249 | `e249_ietf_xor_ic_guard` (limit+1 and `2³²-1` abort for mlen ∈ {65,128,129,192,1024,4096,8192}; legal side for mlen ≤ 64) |\n| 250 | `e250_ietf_xor_ic_guard_underflow_quirk` |\n| 251 | `e251_ietf_xor_mlen_bound` |\n| 253 | `e253_salsa20_no_bounds_check` |\n| 254 | `e254_salsa2012_salsa208_no_bounds_check` |\n| 255 | `e255_xsalsa20_never_fails` |\n| 256 | `e256_xchacha20_delegates` |\n| 257 | `e257_zero_length_writes_nothing` |\n\nAll 70 `crypto_stream*` symbols exported by the C `.so` (verified by an `nm` sweep) plus both stream `_pick_best_implementation` symbols are driven. The only unexported one, `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_ext_xor` (`static` in C), is exercised indirectly through `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_xor`.\n\n## (c) `src/` fix — one edit, one divergence found\n`src/primitives/chacha20.rs:457` in `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_xor_ic`, proven by **ERRORS row 250**:\n- before: `if (ic as u64) > (64u64 * (1u64 << 32)) / 64u64 - (mlen + 63) / 64 {`\n- after: `if (ic as u64) > ((64u64 * (1u64 << 32)) / 64u64).wrapping_sub(mlen.wrapping_add(63) / 64) {`\n\nThe C expression is all-wrapping unsigned 64-bit arithmetic, so for `mlen > 64·2³²` it underflows to `u64::MAX` and the guard **never fires**. The Rust used checked arithmetic: for `mlen = 64·2³²+1, ic = 2³²-1` the C ran on and faulted (`Returned(42)`) while the Rust trapped and aborted (`Signaled(6)`) — i.e. Rust rejected an input the C accepts. Same for `mlen ≥ 2⁶⁴-63`, where C's `mlen + 63` wraps to 62. Verified by reverting the fix (test fails with the exact message above) and re-applying (passes). No other `src/` change was needed; `t00_smoke` still passes.\n\n## (d) Rows not covered / caveats\nNothing is skipped, but two rows required an indirect observation because their thresholds are not allocatable:\n\n- **Rows 245/246/247/252** say \"misuse\", but `SODIUM_SIZE_MAX = min(UINT64_MAX, SIZE_MAX) = UINT64_MAX` on this target and the argument is `unsigned long long`, so `len > MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` is **provably dead code** in the C. The tests assert the bound really is `UINT64_MAX` in both libs and then, in forked children, that `len = 2⁶⁴-1` does **not** produce `SIGABRT` in either lib.\n- **Row 250** is reached *without* allocating 256 GiB: the ic guard is evaluated **before** any memory is touched, so a forked child with a SIGSEGV/SIGBUS handler that `_exit(42)`s cleanly separates \"guard fired\" (`SIGABRT`) from \"guard did not fire, memory access happened\" (`Returned(42)`). This pins the exact arithmetic triple: `(mlen = 64·2³², ic = 0)` → legal, `(64·2³², ic = 1)` → misuse (limit is exactly 0), `(64·2³²+1, ic = 2³²-1)` → **no** misuse (underflow quirk), plus the `mlen+63` wrap cases. The 256 GiB buffer is never allocated.\n\nOne environmental note: forked children now `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, 0)` — without it, `systemd-coredump` dumped every aborting/faulting child and the suite took 100 s instead of 3 s."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":1326859,"totalTokens":173335,"totalToolUseCount":52,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1372,"cache_read_input_tokens":169352,"output_tokens":2609,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1372},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":34,"editFileCount":10,"linesAdded":1856,"linesRemoved":49,"otherToolCount":0}}}
