ust had translated the `HAVE_PTHREAD` branch instead.\n\nNo other `src/` file was changed (`src/utils.rs` and `src/randombytes.rs` were temporarily mutated for a vacuity check and restored byte-identically — `diff` clean).\n\n**Mutation check (proves the tests are not vacuous):** breaking three Rust functions (12-byte `sodium_increment` fast path, `sodium_unpad`'s unconditional out-param write, `randombytes_uniform`'s `min`) made exactly `cfg38`, `err53`, `cfg50` fail and nothing else.\n\n## (d) Rows whose documented branch is unreachable in this build (each still gets a C-vs-Rust assertion of the reachable behaviour, and the reason is a comment in the test)\n- **ERRORS 54** — the `size >= SIZE_MAX - page_size*4` pre-check lives inside `#ifdef HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC`, undefined here (plain `malloc` path). Tested the reachable equivalent: 69 huge sizes → NULL + `errno=ENOMEM`, identical in both.\n- **ERRORS 66** — `sodium_init() == -1` requires `sodium_crit_enter/leave` to fail; in the `#else` branch they cannot. Asserted both helpers return 0 and `sodium_init` never returns −1 in either library.\n- **ERRORS 79** — the `-1` from a second `/dev/urandom` close is unreachable because `getrandom(2)` exists on this kernel, so `stream.getrandom_available != 0` forces `0`. Repeated `randombytes_close()` return values + errno are still compared call-by-call.\n- **ERRORS 76 (latent, not observable)** — `src/randombytes.rs` omits the C's explicit `size > 0x4000000000` → `sodium_misuse()` check, but the downstream `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf` in *both* libraries performs the identical `clen > crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` (= 64·2³² = 0x4000000000) misuse check, so both abort at exactly the same threshold. The test passes with no `src/` edit; flagging it in case you want the redundant check added for literal fidelity.\n\n**Build caveat for the caller:** `cargo test --test t02_utils` does **not** rebuild the cdylib that the harness `dlopen`s — run `cargo build` first after any `src/` change (I hit a stale-`.so` false failure this way). Also note `tests/t01_codecs.rs` (another agent's in-flight file) currently fails, so a bare `cargo test` aborts before reaching t02; run per test target."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa4afc1d9d384c1da (use SendMessage with to: 'aa4afc1d9d384c1da', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 203582\ntool_uses: 63\nduration_ms: 1750392</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"76a32d0a-13eb-452b-94eb-add0f1fd7943","timestamp":"2026-08-14T17:15:08.399Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification of libsodium 1.0.23.\n\nREPO: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nYOUR FILE (create it, nothing else): tests/t02_utils.rs\n\n## HARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify: `c_src/**` (the C is ground truth), `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`, or any other `tests/t*.rs`.\n- You MAY read anything. You MAY edit files under `src/` ONLY to fix a genuine Rust translation bug proven by a failing differential test.\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly — every call goes through `dlsym` on BOTH shared libraries via the harness.\n- NEVER weaken/skip an assertion. The C is ALWAYS correct.\n\n## THE HARNESS (tests/common/mod.rs — read it first)\n```rust\nmod common; use common::*;\ninit_both();\nlet (c, r) = fnpair!(\"symbol_name\", unsafe extern \"C\" fn(A,B)->R);\nunsafe { pair::<T>(\"name\") }\nassert_eq_bytes(\"what\", &c_out, &r_out);\nRng::new(SEED)   // .bytes(n) .byte() .next_u32() .next_u64() .below(n) .fill(&mut b) .pick(&xs)\nforked(|| -> i64 { ... }) -> Outcome    // Outcome::Returned(i64) | Outcome::Signaled(i32); SIGABRT==6\nassert_same_fatal(\"what\", c_outcome, r_outcome);\ninstall_det_rng(with_uniform: bool); reset_det_rng();\nlibs() -> &'static Libs { c: Library, r: Library }\nsym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")   // single-lib lookup; use inside forked closures\npub struct RandombytesImpl { implementation_name, random, stir, uniform, buf, close }  // #[repr(C)]\n```\n`install_det_rng(false)` installs a deterministic counter RNG into BOTH libs with `uniform=NULL` (so libsodium's own rejection sampler in `randombytes_uniform` runs). `install_det_rng(true)` sets `uniform` to a callback (delegation path). The two libs get INDEPENDENT counters that produce the SAME stream, and both are reset. Call `reset_det_rng()` before each paired comparison so C and Rust consume the same stream.\n\nFor abort rows: build a per-library closure, run under `forked(...)`, assert both are `Outcome::Signaled(SIGABRT)` and equal via `assert_same_fatal`.\nFor errno rows: `*libc::__errno_location() = 0;` before, read after; compare C's errno to Rust's.\n\n## WHAT TO COVER\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows **30–53** and `ERRORS.md` rows **48–80** in the repo root — they are your specification. They cover:\n- `c_src/libsodium/sodium/utils.c`: `sodium_pad`, `sodium_unpad`, `sodium_memcmp`, `sodium_compare`, `sodium_is_zero`, `sodium_increment`, `sodium_add`, `sodium_sub`, `sodium_malloc`, `sodium_allocarray`, `sodium_free`, `sodium_mprotect_noaccess/_readonly/_readwrite`, `sodium_mlock`, `sodium_munlock`, `sodium_memzero`, `sodium_stackzero`\n- `c_src/libsodium/sodium/core.c`: `sodium_init`, `sodium_set_misuse_handler`\n- `c_src/libsodium/sodium/runtime.c`: the 12 `sodium_runtime_has_*`\n- `c_src/libsodium/sodium/version.c`\n- `c_src/libsodium/randombytes/**`: `randombytes_buf`, `randombytes_buf_deterministic`, `randombytes_random`, `randombytes_uniform`, `randombytes_stir`, `randombytes_close`, `randombytes_seedbytes`, `randombytes_implementation_name`, `randombytes_set_implementation`, `randombytes` (NaCl compat), and the exported `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` / `randombytes_internal_implementation` structs.\n\nRead `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/utils.h`, `core.h`, `randombytes.h` for exact signatures. Note:\n```c\nint sodium_pad(size_t *padded_buflen_p, unsigned char *buf, size_t unpadded_buflen, size_t blocksize, size_t max_buflen);\nint sodium_unpad(size_t *unpadded_buflen_p, const unsigned char *buf, size_t padded_buflen, size_t blocksize);\nint sodium_compare(const unsigned char *b1_, const unsigned char *b2_, size_t len);  /* LITTLE-ENDIAN, from index len-1 down */\nvoid sodium_increment(unsigned char *n, size_t nlen);\nvoid sodium_add(unsigned char *a, const unsigned char *b, size_t len);\nvoid sodium_sub(unsigned char *a, const unsigned char *b, size_t len);\nvoid randombytes_buf_deterministic(void *buf, size_t size, const unsigned char seed[randombytes_SEEDBYTES]);\n```\n\n### Requirements\n1. **Every** CONFIGS row 30–53 gets a test with ≥64 randomized inputs where a random shape exists.\n2. **Every** ERRORS row 48–80 gets a C-vs-Rust assertion: same return value, same out-params, same errno.\n3 `randombytes_buf_deterministic` is FULLY deterministic — this is the primary byte-for-byte comparison point. Sweep seeds {all-0x00, all-0xff, 0x00..0x1f, ASCII, random×8} × size {0,1,31,32,33,63,64,65,1000,65536}.\n4. `sodium_increment` / `sodium_add` / `sodium_sub`: MUST include len 8, 12, 24 (and 64 for sub) — those are AMD64-asm fast paths in the C, so a portable-vs-asm divergence hides exactly there. Include all-0xff wrap, partial carry, and 0-1 borrow.\n5. `sodium_pad`/`sodium_unpad`: blocksize {1,2,16,256} (power-of-two `&` path) AND {17,255} (non-power-of-two `%` path) × unpadded_buflen {0,1,bs-1,bs,bs+1,2bs}. For ERRORS row 53 assert BOTH that -1 is returned AND that `*unpadded_buflen_p` IS written to `padded_buflen-1`; for rows 48/50/51/52 assert the out-param is NOT written (prefill it with a sentinel and check it is unchanged).\n6. `sodium_init`: careful — the harness already called it. Assert repeated calls return 1 from both libs.\n7. `sodium_runtime_has_*`: compare all 12 between libs.\n8. `randombytes_uniform`: with `install_det_rng(false)` (libsodium's own rejection sampler) sweep upper_bound {0,1,2,3,7,0x7fffffff,0x80000001,0xffffffff} and MANY random bounds; `reset_det_rng()` before each pair so both consume the same stream. Then repeat with `install_det_rng(true)` for the delegation path.\n9. Prefill output buffers with a sentinel (0xAA) and compare the FULL buffer so out-of-range writes are caught.\n10. `sodium_malloc`/`_free`/`_allocarray`/`_mprotect_*`: compare return values / NULL-ness / errno. You cannot compare the pointer VALUES; compare behaviour (non-NULL, prefill byte pattern readable, errno on overflow, ENOSYS from mprotect).\n\n## ON DIVERGENCE\n1. Print the exact inputs, C result and Rust result.\n2. C is in `c_src/libsodium/sodium/{utils,core,runtime,version}.c` and `c_src/libsodium/randombytes/**`; Rust is in `src/utils.rs`, `src/core_init.rs`, `src/runtime.rs`, `src/version.rs`, `src/randombytes.rs`.\n3. Fix the Rust to match the C EXACTLY, including quirks.\n4. Re-run and confirm.\n5. Report EVERY `src/` edit with a one-line before/after and the proving row.\n\n## BUILD / RUN\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test t02_utils 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n\n## DELIVERABLE\n`tests/t02_utils.rs` compiling and PASSING. Report: (a) test count, (b) CONFIGS/ERRORS rows → test fn mapping, (c) every `src/` fix with the proving row, (d) any row not covered and precisely why.","agentId":"aa4afc1d9d384c1da","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`tests/t02_utils.rs` is complete: **41 tests, all passing** (`cargo test --test t02_utils` → `41 passed; 0 failed` in ~15s). `tests/t00_smoke.rs` still passes too.\n\n## (a) Test count\n41 `#[test]` functions, ~2160 lines. Every row with a random shape drives ≥64 randomized inputs (each such test ends with an `assert!(cases >= 64)` self-check).\n\n## (b) Row → test mapping\n| CONFIGS | test fn | | ERRORS | test fn |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|30|`cfg30_pad_power_of_two_blocksizes`| |48|`err48_pad_blocksize_zero`|\n|31|`cfg31_pad_non_power_of_two_blocksizes`| |49|`err49_pad_arith_overflow_is_misuse` (forked, SIGABRT)|\n|32|`cfg32_pad_max_buflen_exact_and_null_outparam`| |50|`err50_pad_xpadded_len_ge_max_buflen` + `cfg32`|\n|33|`cfg33_unpad_padded_len_not_a_multiple_of_blocksize` + round-trip inside `pad_sweep`| |51|`err51_unpad_blocksize_zero`|\n|34|`cfg34_unpad_barrier_at_every_position`| |52|`err52_unpad_padded_shorter_than_blocksize`|\n|35|`cfg35_err62_memcmp`| |53|`err53_unpad_no_barrier_still_writes_outparam` (5 kinds × bs × extra)|\n|36|`cfg36_err63_compare_little_endian` (vs LE model)| |54|`err54_malloc_huge_size_returns_null_enomem`|\n|37|`cfg37_err64_is_zero`| |55|`err55_allocarray_overflow_returns_null_enomem`|\n|38/39/40|`cfg38_increment`, `cfg39_add`, `cfg40_sub` (lens 0,1,2,**8,12**,16,**24**,32,**64**, guarded buffers, vs model)| |56|`err56_free_null_is_noop` (in-process + forked)|\n|41|`cfg41_malloc_free` (0xdb prefill + full read/write)| |57/58/59|`cfg43_err57_58_59_mprotect_lifecycle`|\n|42|`cfg42_allocarray`| |60/61|`err60_61_mlock_munlock` (−1/ENOSYS, munlock zeroes first)|\n|43|`cfg43_err57_58_59_mprotect_lifecycle`| |62/63/64|`cfg35`/`cfg36`/`cfg37`|\n|44|`cfg44_memzero_stackzero`| |65|`cfg45_err65_sodium_init_repeated_returns_1`|\n|45|`cfg45_err65…`, `cfg45_err70_runtime_features_before_init_and_first_init`, `cfg45_runtime_has_all_twelve`| |66|`err66_init_never_returns_minus_one`|\n|46|`cfg46_version`| |67|`err67_crit_leave_while_unlocked` ← **found the bug**|\n|47|`cfg47_randombytes_buf_deterministic_sweep` (12 seeds × 10 sizes, byte-for-byte)| |68|`err68_misuse_runs_handler_then_always_aborts` (returning handler → SIGABRT; `_exit(37)` handler proves it runs; no handler → SIGABRT)|\n|48|`cfg48_err72_err80_default_impl_and_seedbytes`| |69|`err69_set_misuse_handler_null_returns_0`|\n|49|`cfg49_err71_err77_injected_impl_random_and_buf`| |70|`cfg45_err70_…`|\n|50|`cfg50_err73_err74_uniform_rejection_sampler` (147 bounds + 512 lockstep)| |71/72|`cfg49…` / `cfg48…`|\n|51|`cfg51_err75_uniform_delegation`| |73/74/75|`cfg50…` / `cfg51…`|\n|52|`cfg52_err78_err79_stir_and_close`| |76|`err76_buf_deterministic_size_over_2pow38_is_misuse`|\n|53|`cfg53_nacl_compat_and_internal_implementation` (+ NaCl `randombytes()` in `cfg49`)| |77/78/79/80|`cfg49…` / `cfg52…` / `cfg52…` / `cfg47`+`cfg48`|\n\nNotable techniques: all output buffers are 0xAA-prefilled with 16-byte head/tail guards and compared in full; `size_t` out-params are prefilled with `0xDEADBEEFDEADBEEF`; every RNG-mutating test holds a file-local mutex (cargo runs tests as parallel threads over one shared pair of `.so`s) and restores each library's own `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` afterwards.\n\n## (c) `src/` fixes\n**One edit — `src/core_init.rs`** (proving row: ERRORS 67, `err67_crit_leave_while_unlocked`, which reported `C=0 rust=-1`):\n- before: `sodium_crit_enter` = `pthread_mutex_lock(...)` + `LOCKED=1`; `sodium_crit_leave` = `if LOCKED==0 { errno=EPERM; return -1 } LOCKED=0; pthread_mutex_unlock(...)`.\n- after: both are unconditional `0`.\n- why: `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` defines no `HAVE_*` macros (`flags.make` = `-Dsodium_EXPORTS` only; `nm -D --undefined-only` shows no `pthread_*`), so core.c compiles the final `#else` branch where both helpers `return 0` and `locked` is never touched. The Rust had translated the `HAVE_PTHREAD` branch instead.\n\nNo other `src/` file was changed (`src/utils.rs` and `src/randombytes.rs` were temporarily mutated for a vacuity check and restored byte-identically — `diff` clean).\n\n**Mutation check (proves the tests are not vacuous):** breaking three Rust functions (12-byte `sodium_increment` fast path, `sodium_unpad`'s unconditional out-param write, `randombytes_uniform`'s `min`) made exactly `cfg38`, `err53`, `cfg50` fail and nothing else.\n\n## (d) Rows whose documented branch is unreachable in this build (each still gets a C-vs-Rust assertion of the reachable behaviour, and the reason is a comment in the test)\n- **ERRORS 54** — the `size >= SIZE_MAX - page_size*4` pre-check lives inside `#ifdef HAVE_ALIGNED_MALLOC`, undefined here (plain `malloc` path). Tested the reachable equivalent: 69 huge sizes → NULL + `errno=ENOMEM`, identical in both.\n- **ERRORS 66** — `sodium_init() == -1` requires `sodium_crit_enter/leave` to fail; in the `#else` branch they cannot. Asserted both helpers return 0 and `sodium_init` never returns −1 in either library.\n- **ERRORS 79** — the `-1` from a second `/dev/urandom` close is unreachable because `getrandom(2)` exists on this kernel, so `stream.getrandom_available != 0` forces `0`. Repeated `randombytes_close()` return values + errno are still compared call-by-call.\n- **ERRORS 76 (latent, not observable)** — `src/randombytes.rs` omits the C's explicit `size > 0x4000000000` → `sodium_misuse()` check, but the downstream `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf` in *both* libraries performs the identical `clen > crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` (= 64·2³² = 0x4000000000) misuse check, so both abort at exactly the same threshold. The test passes with no `src/` edit; flagging it in case you want the redundant check added for literal fidelity.\n\n**Build caveat for the caller:** `cargo test --test t02_utils` does **not** rebuild the cdylib that the harness `dlopen`s — run `cargo build` first after any `src/` change (I hit a stale-`.so` false failure this way). Also note `tests/t01_codecs.rs` (another agent's in-flight file) currently fails, so a bare `cargo test` aborts before reaching t02; run per test target."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":1750392,"totalTokens":203582,"totalToolUseCount":63,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3134,"cache_read_input_tokens":197312,"output_tokens":3135,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":183},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"se