n| removed `hex_pos -= 1` | `errors_03_to_10` (ERRORS-3) + `configs_08`: `*hex_end C=Some(2) rust=Some(3)` |\n| write one NUL instead of zero-filling to `b64_maxlen` | `configs_15`: whole-buffer OUTPUT MISMATCH |\n| zero-run tie-break `>` → `>=` | `configs_27_28`: OUTPUT MISMATCH |\n\n## (d) Rows not covered by a `dlsym` call, and why\n\n1. **CONFIGS row 10's `sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN`** — it is a preprocessor macro in `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/utils.h`, not an exported symbol, so it cannot be reached via `dlsym`. Covered indirectly: `expected_encoded_len()` re-derives the documented table independently and the C function is asserted equal to it for all 4 variants × `bin_len` 0..40 plus 64 random lengths up to 100 000, and cross-checked against `strlen(sodium_bin2base64(...)) + 1`.\n\nEverything else in CONFIGS 1–29 / ERRORS 1–47 is covered by a real `dlsym` call on both libraries. Two secondary notes:\n\n2. **The prompt's `sodium_ip2bin` sketch is wrong** — the real signature has no `bin_maxlen`: `int sodium_ip2bin(unsigned char bin[16], const char *ip, size_t ip_len_)`, and `char *sodium_bin2ip(char *ip, size_t ip_maxlen, const unsigned char bin[16])`. I used the header's signatures.\n3. **Fork hygiene was required to make the misuse rows work at all.** Initially the misuse test *hung*: a forked child of the multi-threaded test process deadlocked on the loader/malloc lock because the child did `dlsym` + `Vec` allocation. All symbol lookup, allocation and `format!` now happen in the parent; the child only calls a pre-resolved function pointer. I also set `RLIMIT_CORE = 0` in that test — without it the ~800 `SIGABRT`s are piped to `systemd-coredump`, which took the runtime from 36 s to 164 s."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a00a2aa8f58a8548c (use SendMessage with to: 'a00a2aa8f58a8548c', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 213879\ntool_uses: 78\nduration_ms: 2987428</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"00ab9d12-0714-4fa3-a17e-2deb64a5b312","timestamp":"2026-08-14T17:35:15.323Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification of libsodium 1.0.23.\n\nREPO: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nYOUR FILE (create it, nothing else): tests/t01_codecs.rs\n\n## HARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify: `c_src/**` (the C is ground truth), `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`, or any other `tests/t*.rs`.\n- You MAY read anything. You MAY edit files under `src/` ONLY to fix a genuine Rust translation bug you have proven with a failing differential test (see \"ON DIVERGENCE\").\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly. Every call goes through `dlsym` on BOTH shared libraries via the harness. This is what tests the `#[no_mangle]` export wrappers.\n- NEVER weaken/skip an assertion to make a test pass. The C is ALWAYS correct.\n\n## THE HARNESS (tests/common/mod.rs — already written, read it first)\n```rust\nmod common; use common::*;\ninit_both();                       // sodium_init() on both libs, once\nlet (c, r) = fnpair!(\"symbol_name\", unsafe extern \"C\" fn(A,B)->R);  // Symbol pair\nunsafe { pair::<T>(\"name\") }       // same thing, non-macro\nassert_eq_bytes(\"what\", &c_out, &r_out);  // byte-for-byte, pretty diff\nRng::new(SEED)                     // SplitMix64; .bytes(n) .byte() .next_u32() .below(n) .fill(&mut b) .pick(&xs)\npub const SEED: u64                // fixed seed => reproducible\nforked(|| -> i64 { ... }) -> Outcome     // run in a forked child\nOutcome::Returned(i64) | Outcome::Signaled(i32);  pub const SIGABRT: i32 = 6;\nassert_same_fatal(\"what\", c_outcome, r_outcome);  // C and Rust abort identically\ninstall_det_rng(with_uniform: bool); reset_det_rng();  // inject deterministic RNG into BOTH libs\nlibs() -> &'static Libs { c: Library, r: Library }   // for per-lib forked calls\nsym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")        // single-lib lookup (use inside forked closures)\n```\nFor a `misuse`/abort row: build a closure per library that calls the function, run it under `forked(...)`, and assert BOTH give `Outcome::Signaled(SIGABRT)` AND that they are equal via `assert_same_fatal`.\nFor errno rows: call `*libc::__errno_location() = 0;` before the call, read it after. Compare C's errno to Rust's errno.\n\n## WHAT TO COVER\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows **1–29** and `ERRORS.md` rows **1–47** in the repo root — they are your specification. They cover `c_src/libsodium/sodium/codecs.c`:\nhex (`sodium_bin2hex`, `sodium_hex2bin`), base64 (`sodium_base64_encoded_len`, `sodium_bin2base64`, `sodium_base642bin`) and IP (`sodium_ip2bin`, `sodium_bin2ip`).\n\nAlso read `c_src/libsodium/sodium/codecs.c` and `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/utils.h` for exact signatures. Key signatures:\n```c\nchar *sodium_bin2hex(char *hex, size_t hex_maxlen, const unsigned char *bin, size_t bin_len);\nint sodium_hex2bin(unsigned char *bin, size_t bin_maxlen, const char *hex, size_t hex_len,\n                   const char *ignore, size_t *bin_len, const char **hex_end);\nsize_t sodium_base64_encoded_len(size_t bin_len, int variant);\nchar *sodium_bin2base64(char *b64, size_t b64_maxlen, const unsigned char *bin, size_t bin_len, int variant);\nint sodium_base642bin(unsigned char *bin, size_t bin_maxlen, const char *b64, size_t b64_len,\n                      const char *ignore, size_t *bin_len, const char **b64_end, int variant);\nint sodium_ip2bin(unsigned char *bin, size_t bin_maxlen, const char *ip, size_t ip_len_, ...);  /* CHECK the real signature in the header */\nchar *sodium_bin2ip(char *ip, size_t ip_maxlen, const unsigned char *bin, size_t bin_len);      /* CHECK */\n```\nVariants: `sodium_base64_VARIANT_ORIGINAL=1, ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING=3, URLSAFE=5, URLSAFE_NO_PADDING=7`.\n\n### Requirements per row\n1. **Every** CONFIGS row 1–29 gets a `#[test]` (or a clearly-named section of one) driving ≥64 randomized inputs where a random shape exists, using `Rng::new(SEED)`.\n2. **Every** ERRORS row 1–47 gets an assertion comparing C vs Rust: same return value, same `*bin_len`/`*hex_end`/`*b64_end` out-params, AND same `errno`. Note the rows where errno is deliberately UNCHANGED (18, 19) — assert it is unchanged in BOTH.\n3. For `sodium_bin2base64`, compare the WHOLE `b64_maxlen`-byte output buffer, not just up to the NUL — the C zero-fills the remainder.\n4. For the invalid-variant rows, test variant values `0,2,4,6,8,9,99,0xFFFFFFFFu32 as i32` — these are out-of-range enums crossing FFI and must abort identically.\n5. Include round-trip tests (bin2hex→hex2bin; bin2base64→base642bin for all 4 variants × bin_len 0..64).\n6. Prefill every output buffer with a known sentinel (e.g. 0xAA) and compare the FULL buffer afterwards so out-of-range writes are caught.\n\n## ON DIVERGENCE\nWhen C and Rust disagree:\n1. Print the exact inputs, the C result and the Rust result.\n2. Find the C code in `c_src/libsodium/sodium/codecs.c` and the Rust in `src/codecs.rs`.\n3. Fix `src/codecs.rs` so it matches the C EXACTLY (including quirks: errno left unset, partial `*bin_len` retained, ERANGE overwritten by EINVAL, the embedded-NUL/`strchr(ignore,0)` quirk, `'='`-in-ignore behaviour).\n4. Re-run and confirm.\n5. In your final report, list EVERY `src/` edit you made with a one-line before/after and which ERRORS/CONFIGS row proved it.\n\n## BUILD / RUN\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test t01_codecs 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nThe C `.so` is already built at `c_src/build/libsodium.so`. `cargo test` rebuilds the Rust cdylib automatically.\n\n## DELIVERABLE\n`tests/t01_codecs.rs` compiling and PASSING. Final report: (a) test count, (b) which CONFIGS/ERRORS rows are covered by which test fn, (c) every `src/` fix with the proving row, (d) any row you could NOT cover and precisely why.","agentId":"a00a2aa8f58a8548c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 20 tests pass. Final report:\n\n## (a) Deliverable & counts\n\n`tests/t01_codecs.rs` (110 KB, 20 `#[test]` functions) — **20 passed, 0 failed** in ~36 s.\n\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\ncargo build && cargo test --test t01_codecs\n```\n\n**Critical build finding:** `cargo test` alone does **not** rebuild `target/debug/liblibsodium.so`. The crate is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` only, and an integration-test target has no dependency on the cdylib artifact, so `cargo test` silently runs against a **stale `.so`**. I caught this during mutation validation (4 deliberate bugs in `src/codecs.rs` produced 20/20 PASS until I ran `cargo build`). `cargo build &&` is mandatory; this is documented at the top of the test file.\n\n## (b) Row → test-function coverage\n\n| CONFIGS rows | test fn |\n|---|---|\n| 1, 2 | `configs_01_02_bin2hex_exact_and_oversized_hex_maxlen` |\n| 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 | `configs_03_to_07_hex2bin_valid_shapes_and_hex_end` (384 generated cases × {hex_end NULL/non-NULL} × {bin_len NULL/non-NULL}) |\n| 8 | `configs_08_hex2bin_oversized_bin_maxlen_and_hex_len_shapes` |\n| 9 | `configs_09_hex_roundtrip` |\n| 10 | `configs_10_base64_encoded_len_table` |\n| 11, 12, 13, 14 | `configs_11_to_14_bin2base64_all_variants_exact_maxlen` |\n| 15 | `configs_15_bin2base64_oversized_maxlen_zero_fill` |\n| 16, 17, 18 | `configs_16_17_18_base642bin_valid_decodes` |\n| 19 | `configs_19_base642bin_ignore_whitespace` |\n| 20 | `configs_20_base642bin_letter_a_shapes` |\n| 21 | `configs_21_base64_roundtrip_all_variants` |\n| 22 | `configs_22_ip2bin_ipv4_dotted_quad` |\n| 23, 24, 25, 26 | `configs_23_24_25_26_ip2bin_ipv6_zone_and_len` |\n| 27, 28 | `configs_27_28_bin2ip_maxlen_sweep_and_zero_run` |\n| 29 | `configs_29_ip_roundtrip_canonicalisation` |\n\n| ERRORS rows | test fn |\n|---|---|\n| 1, 2, 11, 12, 13, 14 | `errors_01_02_11_12_13_14_misuse_branches` (~800 forks; all 8 bad variants × 3 entry points, plus non-abort *controls* so the misuse rows can't pass vacuously) |\n| 3–10 | `errors_03_to_10_hex2bin` |\n| 15–30 | `errors_15_to_30_base642bin` |\n| 31–45 | `errors_31_to_45_ip2bin` (+1024 mutation/random-string fuzz cases) |\n| 46, 47 | `errors_46_47_bin2ip` |\n\nEvery row drives ≥64 randomized inputs from `Rng::new(SEED)` where a random shape exists; every output buffer is 0xAA-prefilled and compared in full (including slack past the declared max) via `assert_eq_bytes`; every `sodium_hex2bin`/`sodium_base642bin` call compares return value, full `bin` buffer, `*bin_len`, `*hex_end`/`*b64_end` offset **and** `errno` (primed with a 0x5EED marker, so rows 18/19's \"errno UNCHANGED\" is a positive assertion).\n\n## (c) `src/` fixes\n\n**None. Zero divergences found** — `src/codecs.rs` is byte-identical to its original state (md5 `63ec37d3293e8aa7a510a283d4f423bf`); no other file under `src/`, `c_src/`, `tests/` or the root `.md`/`Cargo.toml` was modified.\n\nAll the documented quirks are already faithfully reproduced and were asserted identical on both `.so`s: ERANGE-overwritten-by-EINVAL (rows 8, 21), partial `*bin_len` retained (rows 4, 15, 24), errno left untouched on the `acc_len > 4` / nonzero-trailing-bits branch (rows 18, 19), the `strchr(ignore, 0)` embedded-NUL skip (row 10), `'='`-in-`ignore` → ERANGE (row 28), NO_PADDING fed padding (rows 24, 25), `hex_pos--` on odd digit count (row 3), and `b64[b64_len..b64_maxlen)` zero-fill (row 15).\n\nBecause \"no divergence\" is only meaningful if the tests bite, I validated with 4 temporary mutations of `src/codecs.rs`, all caught by the *differential* assertions (not just my expectation asserts), then restored the file:\n\n| injected bug | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| set `errno = EINVAL` on the `acc_len > 4` branch | `errors_15_to_30` (ERRORS-18) + `configs_20`: `errno C=24301 rust=22` |\n| removed `hex_pos -= 1` | `errors_03_to_10` (ERRORS-3) + `configs_08`: `*hex_end C=Some(2) rust=Some(3)` |\n| write one NUL instead of zero-filling to `b64_maxlen` | `configs_15`: whole-buffer OUTPUT MISMATCH |\n| zero-run tie-break `>` → `>=` | `configs_27_28`: OUTPUT MISMATCH |\n\n## (d) Rows not covered by a `dlsym` call, and why\n\n1. **CONFIGS row 10's `sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN`** — it is a preprocessor macro in `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/utils.h`, not an exported symbol, so it cannot be reached via `dlsym`. Covered indirectly: `expected_encoded_len()` re-derives the documented table independently and the C function is asserted equal to it for all 4 variants × `bin_len` 0..40 plus 64 random lengths up to 100 000, and cross-checked against `strlen(sodium_bin2base64(...)) + 1`.\n\nEverything else in CONFIGS 1–29 / ERRORS 1–47 is covered by a real `dlsym` call on both libraries. Two secondary notes:\n\n2. **The prompt's `sodium_ip2bin` sketch is wrong** — the real signature has no `bin_maxlen`: `int sodium_ip2bin(unsigned char bin[16], const char *ip, size_t ip_len_)`, and `char *sodium_bin2ip(char *ip, size_t ip_maxlen, const unsigned char bin[16])`. I used the header's signatures.\n3. **Fork hygiene was required to make the misuse rows work at all.** Initially the misuse test *hung*: a forked child of the multi-threaded test process deadlocked on the loader/malloc lock because the child did `dlsym` + `Vec` allocation. All symbol lookup, allocation and `format!` now happen in the parent; the child only calls a pre-resolved function pointer. I also set `RLIMIT_CORE = 0` in that test — without it the ~800 `SIGABRT`s are piped to `systemd-coredump`, which took the runtime from 36 s to 164 s."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":2987428,"totalTokens":213879,"totalToolUseCount":78,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":861,"cache_read_input_tokens":208431,"output_tokens":4585,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1955},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetc