{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae1738d421a430298","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_013xj565Xgh7bG77qAPP4THZ","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-nOS43A-translated-rust/94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051/tasks/ae1738d421a430298.output","summary":"Done. `tests/t05_mac.rs` (2303 lines) compiles and passes: **31 tests, 0 failures** (~3.2 s).\n\n## (a) Test count\n31 `#[test]` functions. Every one that drives a CONFIGS row ends with an `assert!(iters >= 64, ...)` counter so it can't silently shrink below the required 64 randomized inputs.\n\n## (b) Row → test mapping\n\n| rows | test fn |\n|---|---|\n| C98 | `r98_poly1305_oneshot` |\n| C99 | `r99_poly1305_leftover_axis` (15 named scripts covering branches a–e) |\n| C100 | `r100_poly1305_update_chunkings` (1+15, 15+1, 16, 8+8, 17, 31+1, 32, 33, 0-len) |\n| C101 | `r101_poly1305_final_leftover` (leftover 0..15, single-call vs 1-byte dribble) |\n| C102 | `r102_onetimeauth_dispatch` (+`_crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_pick_best_implementation`, called before/after use) |\n| C103/104 | `r103_hmacsha256_init_keylen_axis`, `r104_hmacsha256_streaming_vs_oneshot` |\n| C105/106 | `r105_hmacsha512_init_keylen_axis`, `r106_hmacsha512_streaming_vs_oneshot` |\n| C107 | `r107_hmacsha512256_streaming_and_truncation` (asserts the 512256 state is byte-identical to a 512 state and the tag is `hmacsha512[..32]`) |\n| C108 | `r108_crypto_auth_dispatch` |\n| C109 + E157–160 | `r109_e157_e160_all_verify_byte_positions` |\n| C110 + C121 | `r110_r121_keygen_all` (mutex-guarded `install_det_rng`, restores `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` in *both* libs) |\n| C111–118 | `r111_…unkeyed`, `r112_…keyed`, `r113_e230_…`, `r114_…salt_personal_oneshot`, `r115_…streaming_unkeyed`, `r116_…streaming_keyed`, `r117_…update_multichunk`, `r118_…init_salt_personal_streaming` |\n| C119 + E234 | `r119_e234_blake2b_final_outlen_mismatch` |\n| C120 + E235/236 | `r120_e235_e236_generichash_dispatch` |\n| E154–156 | `e154_e156_hmac_init_null_key_misuse` (`forked`, asserts `Signaled(SIGABRT)`, plus a `keylen==0` control that must `Returned(0)`) |\n| E161 | `e161_poly1305_verify_mismatch` |\n| E222–225 | `e222_e224_e225_generichash_oneshot_bounds` |\n| E226 / E227–229 / E231 | `e226_salt_personal_bounds`, `e227_e229_generichash_init_bounds`, `e231_init_salt_personal_bounds` (all assert the rejected `_init` leaves all 512 state bytes at 0xAA) |\n| E230 | `r113_e230_null_key_positive_keylen` |\n| E232 | `e232_generichash_final_bad_outlen_misuse` (`forked`; + legal-outlen controls that must `Returned(0)`) |\n| E233 | `e233_generichash_final_twice` |\n\nEvery state is a 512-byte, 64-byte-aligned 0xAA buffer; `assert_statebytes()` cross-checks each `*_statebytes()` (256/208/416/416/384/384) and asserts `SB ≥ statebytes+64`. Whole-buffer snapshots are taken after `_init` **and after every op** and compared. All output buffers carry a 32-byte 0xAA guard checked on *both* sides. Error rows run against both API layers (`crypto_generichash_blake2b*` and `crypto_generichash*`) for ERRORS 235.\n\n## (c) `src/` fixes\n**None — zero divergences found.** The Rust translation matched the C byte-for-byte on every return code, digest, output guard and full opaque-state snapshot, including all the quirks in the brief (poly1305 `init` leaving `buffer[16]` untouched at 0xAA and `finish` zeroing exactly 144 bytes; HMAC key pre-hashing through `state->ictx`; BLAKE2b's 361-of-384 written bytes; sub-`_MIN` outlen/keylen acceptance; `_final` misuse-not-`-1`; double-`_final` → `-1`; outlen mismatch silently returning 0).\n\n## (d) Coverage caveats\n1. **`crypto_generichash_blake2b_final` with `outlen ≥ 256` is intentionally not asserted.** The build defines no `NDEBUG`, so `assert(outlen <= UINT8_MAX)` in `generichash_blake2b.c:107` is live: C would abort via `assert`, Rust via `(u8)outlen == 0` reaching `sodium_misuse`. Both are SIGABRT, but the row would be testing the C `assert` rather than the translation, so ERRORS 232 is driven with `outlen ∈ {0, 65, 66, 100, 128, 254, 255}` (all of which pass the assert and genuinely reach `blake2b_final`'s guard). `outlen == 256` *is* exercised on the `_init`/one-shot/`salt_personal` paths, where the `> 64` check returns `-1` before the assert.\n2. **CONFIGS row 113 is inaccurate as written** and the test asserts the real C behaviour instead: `key == NULL && keylen > 0` takes the unkeyed path only at the **`_init`** wrappers. The **one-shot** `crypto_generichash_blake2b` / `crypto_generichash` / `..._salt_personal` hit `blake2b()`'s own `NULL == key && keylen > 0` guard and `sodium_misuse()`. Both libraries abort identically (verified via `forked`, `Signaled(SIGABRT)`); the row therefore covers *both* halves.\n3. Row 114's \"distinct salt/personal must change the digest\" is only asserted at `outlen ≥ 16`; at `outlen ∈ 1..15` a chance collision is expected, so those outlens instead run every salt×personal pair through the C-vs-Rust byte comparison.\n\n## Mutation check — 7 performed, all caught, all reverted\n| mutation | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| `poly1305_update`: `s.leftover += want` → `= want` | `r99`, `r100`, `r101` |\n| `hmacsha256_init`: `keylen > 64` → `> 63` | `r103` |\n| `blake2b_final`: `buflen > 128` → `>= 128` | `r111`,`r112`,`r114`–`r118`,`r120` |\n| `crypto_generichash_blake2b_final`: return `-1` instead of reaching the misuse guard | `e232` |\n| `blake2b_final`: drop the `is_lastblock → -1` guard | `e233` |\n| `hmacsha512256_final`: `memcpy` 32 → 31 | `r107`, `r108`, `r109` |\n| `poly1305_init`: don't initialise `state->final` (state snapshot) | `r98`–`r101` |\n\nSources verified byte-identical to their originals afterwards (`diff -q` clean, no `MUTANT` markers remain), and `t00_smoke` + `t05_mac` both pass on the rebuilt `.so`. One leftover artifact: `/tmp/t05bak/` (my source backups) — I was denied permission to `rm` it; it is outside the repo and harmless.","usage":{"total_tokens":200214,"tool_uses":64,"duration_ms":1083195},"uuid":"71954f13-5abf-4b49-859e-b0b4ffdfc17d","session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051"}
