ternm`, `..._zerobytes`, `..._boxzerobytes`, `..._open` and the bare `crypto_box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305` are missing from **both** `.so`s, while the xsalsa20 primitive-level equivalents are present in both.\n- Sealed-box structure is verified constructively, not just by round-trip: the det-RNG is rewound, the ephemeral `esk` is read out of the stream via the library's own `randombytes_buf`, then `epk == scalarmult_base(esk)`, `nonce == BLAKE2b-24(epk‖pk)` and `c[32..] == crypto_box_easy(m, nonce, pk, esk)` are all asserted through both libs.\n- No row is uncovered.\n\n## Mutation checks (7 run, 6 detected, 1 provably equivalent)\n| # | mutation in `src/aead/cryptobox.rs` | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | xsalsa20 `_beforenm` uses `crypto_core_hchacha20` | **caught** — `cfg205_cfg212_beforenm_construction` |\n| M2 | `crypto_box_seal` writes `epk` only when `ret == 0` | **caught** — `e143_seal_low_order_pk_epk_still_written` |\n| M3 | `crypto_box_seal_open` guard `clen < 48` → `clen < 32` | **not caught — equivalent mutant.** For `clen ∈ [32,48)` the inner `crypto_box_open_easy` sees `clen−32 < 16` and returns −1 anyway, so the observable behaviour is identical to the C. Not a real defect. |\n| M3′ | `crypto_box_seal_open` nonce args swapped (`pk‖epk`) | **caught** — `cfg210_seal_seal_open` |\n| M4 | `crypto_box_easy_afternm` guard `>` → `>=` (off-by-one on MESSAGEBYTES_MAX) | **caught** — `e133_e134_easy_messagebytes_max` |\n| M5 | `_seed_keypair` copies `SHA-512(seed)[32..64)` | **caught** — `cfg202_seed_keypair_deterministic` |\n| M6 | `crypto_box_detached` returns 0 instead of −1 on `_beforenm` failure | **caught** — `e132_e135_e136_low_order_easy_detached` |\n| M7 | `crypto_box_easy` swaps the mac/ciphertext destinations | **caught** — `cfg204_detached_open_detached` |\n\nBuild/run command used: `cargo build && cargo test --test t08_box` (with `ulimit -c 0`; fork-heavy tests also `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, 0)` in the child). `install_det_rng` users are serialised by a file-local `Mutex` and restore `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` on the way out."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7c9dc99c15846e9a (use SendMessage with to: 'a7c9dc99c15846e9a', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 189365\ntool_uses: 43\nduration_ms: 1099892</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"69deeeaf-1c11-453e-89b6-ddea55cf25b8","timestamp":"2026-08-14T17:57:39.014Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification of libsodium 1.0.23.\n\nREPO: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nYOUR FILE (create it, nothing else): tests/t08_box.rs\n\n## HARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify: `c_src/**` (the C is ground truth), `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`, `run_difftests.sh`, or any other `tests/t*.rs`.\n- You MAY read anything. You MAY edit files under `src/` ONLY to fix a genuine Rust translation bug proven by a failing differential test.\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly — every call goes through `dlsym` on BOTH shared libraries via the harness.\n- NEVER weaken/skip an assertion. The C is ALWAYS correct. If C and Rust disagree, the RUST is wrong.\n\n## BUILD (critical)\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib for integration tests. ALWAYS:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nulimit -c 0\ncargo build && timeout 600 cargo test --test t08_box 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n\n## THE HARNESS (tests/common/mod.rs — READ IT FIRST)\n```rust\nmod common; use common::*;\ninit_both();\nlet (c, r) = fnpair!(\"symbol_name\", unsafe extern \"C\" fn(A,B)->R);\nassert_eq_bytes(\"what\", &c_out, &r_out);\nRng::new(SEED); pub const LENS: &[usize]; pub fn patterns(n, &mut rng) -> Vec<Vec<u8>>\nforked(|| -> i64 { ... }) -> Outcome; Outcome::Signaled(SIGABRT); assert_same_fatal(..)\ninstall_det_rng(bool); reset_det_rng();\nlibs(); sym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")\n```\n**Fork hygiene**: resolve symbols and allocate buffers in the PARENT; the child closure only calls a pre-resolved fn pointer. `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE,0)` in fork-heavy tests.\n**Parallelism**: tests are parallel threads over one pair of `.so`s; guard `install_det_rng` users with a file-local `Mutex` and restore the default impl after. NOTE: `crypto_box_keypair` and `crypto_box_seal` consume randomness, so they need `install_det_rng` + `reset_det_rng()` before each paired call to be byte-comparable.\n\n## WHAT TO COVER\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows **202–216** and `ERRORS.md` rows **131–153** — they are your specification. EVERY API shape of both primitives:\n\n**`crypto_box` (primitive `curve25519xsalsa20poly1305`)**:\n`_seedbytes`, `_publickeybytes`, `_secretkeybytes`, `_beforenmbytes`, `_noncebytes`, `_macbytes`, `_messagebytes_max`, `_primitive`, `_sealbytes`, `_zerobytes`, `_boxzerobytes`, `_seed_keypair`, `_keypair`, `_beforenm`, `_easy`, `_open_easy`, `_detached`, `_open_detached`, `_easy_afternm`, `_open_easy_afternm`, `_detached_afternm`, `_open_detached_afternm`, `_afternm`, `_open_afternm`, `_seal`, `_seal_open`, and the deprecated `crypto_box` / `crypto_box_open`.\n\n**`crypto_box_curve25519xsalsa20poly1305_*`** — the same primitive-level set (note it HAS `_afternm`/`_open_afternm` and the padded NaCl API).\n\n**`crypto_box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305_*`**: `_seed_keypair`, `_keypair`, `_beforenm`, `_easy`, `_open_easy`, `_detached`, `_open_detached`, `_easy_afternm`, `_open_easy_afternm`, `_detached_afternm`, `_open_detached_afternm`, `_seal`, `_seal_open`, and all constant getters. NOTE: this primitive has **NO** plain `_afternm`/`_open_afternm` and **NO** padded NaCl API — verify those symbols are absent from BOTH libs.\n\nC sources: `c_src/libsodium/crypto_box/**`. Headers: `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/crypto_box*.h`. READ them for exact signatures.\n\n### Critical C behaviours to match\n- `_beforenm` = `hsalsa20(scalarmult(sk,pk), zero[16])` for xsalsa20; **HChacha20** for the xchacha20 primitive.\n- `_detached_afternm` / `_open_detached_afternm` are literal aliases of `crypto_secretbox_detached` / `_open_detached`, so they inherit the 4-way OVERLAP axis (disjoint / in-place / partial-forward / partial-backward) and the `mlen0 = min(mlen,32)` first-block axis — CONFIGS 206. Exercise all four overlap shapes via raw pointers into one big buffer.\n- `_seal`: nonce = BLAKE2b-24(`epk || recipient_pk`), ephemeral pk prepended, SEALBYTES = 48. ERRORS 143: when the recipient pk is a LOW-ORDER point, `_seal` returns -1 **but `c[0..32)` has still been overwritten with `epk`** — assert the buffer contents, not just the return code.\n- `_beforenm` returns -1 when `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519` yields an all-zero shared secret, i.e. the pk is a low-order point (ERRORS 131). Get the 7 blocklisted curve25519 encodings from `has_small_order`'s table in `c_src/libsodium/crypto_scalarmult/curve25519/ref10/x25519_ref10.c` and hard-code them. Drive every function that calls `_beforenm` with such a pk: `_easy`, `_open_easy`, `_detached`, `_open_detached`, `crypto_box`, `crypto_box_open`, `_seal`, and the xchacha20 equivalents.\n- deprecated NaCl API: requires `mlen >= 32` (ZEROBYTES) with 32 leading zero bytes in `m`, and writes 16 leading zero bytes in `c`; `mlen < 32` → -1 (ERRORS 140/141).\n- `_easy`/`_easy_afternm` with `mlen > MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` → `sodium_misuse()` → SIGABRT (ERRORS 133/134/147/148). `MESSAGEBYTES_MAX = SODIUM_SIZE_MAX - 16 = UINT64_MAX - 16` on this target, so that branch may be provably dead — if so, assert the constant's value in both libs and assert that `mlen = UINT64_MAX - 16` does NOT abort in either, and say so in your report.\n- `_open_easy` / `_open_easy_afternm` with `clen < 16` → -1; `_seal_open` with `clen < 48` → -1.\n- `_seed_keypair`: `sk = SHA-512(seed)[0..32)` then `scalarmult_base` — fully deterministic, so it is directly byte-comparable without the RNG shim.\n\n### Requirements\n1. **Every** CONFIGS row 202–216 gets a `#[test]` (or clearly-named section) driving ≥64 randomized inputs.\n2. **Every** ERRORS row 131–153 gets a C-vs-Rust assertion on the return value AND the full output buffer AND errno where applicable.\n3. `mlen` sweep {0,1,31,32,33,64,1000} for the easy/detached/seal shapes; {32,33,64,96,1000} for the padded NaCl shapes.\n4. ECDH agreement property (CONFIGS 215): A→B and B→A give the same `_beforenm` key, for BOTH primitives, asserted in both libs and compared across libs.\n5. Round-trips: `_easy`→`_open_easy`, `_detached`→`_open_detached`, `_easy_afternm`→`_open_easy_afternm`, `_seal`→`_seal_open`, `crypto_box`→`crypto_box_open`, for both primitives.\n6. Tamper tests: corrupt the MAC / each ciphertext byte / the ephemeral pk in a sealed box, and assert both libs reject identically.\n7. `m == NULL` verify-only mode on `_open_detached` / `_open_detached_afternm`.\n8. Prefill every output buffer with 0xAA plus a trailing guard; compare the FULL buffer and assert the guard is intact.\n\n## ON DIVERGENCE\n1. Print exact inputs (pk, sk, nonce, mlen) and the first differing byte offset.\n2. Rust sources: `src/aead/cryptobox.rs`, `src/aead/secretbox.rs`, `src/aead/core_hsalsa20.rs`, `src/ed25519/x25519.rs`.\n3. Fix the Rust to match the C EXACTLY, including the \"epk still written on failure\" behaviour.\n4. Re-run and confirm.\n5. Report EVERY `src/` edit with a one-line before/after and the proving row.\n\n## DELIVERABLE\n`tests/t08_box.rs` compiling and PASSING. Report: (a) test count, (b) CONFIGS/ERRORS rows → test fn mapping, (c) every `src/` fix with the proving row, (d) any row not covered and precisely why. Also run ≥5 mutation checks and report them.","agentId":"a7c9dc99c15846e9a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 33 tests pass; whole suite green (191 tests across 8 binaries).\n\n## Deliverable\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust/tests/t08_box.rs` — 2583 lines, **33 `#[test]` functions**, all passing. No other file was modified.\n\n## (a) Test count\n33 tests. Every property row drives ≥64 randomized inputs (asserted in-test via `assert!(n >= 64)`); the error rows sweep the full boundary sets.\n\n## (b) Row → test mapping\n| CONFIGS | test |\n|---|---|\n| 202 | `cfg202_seed_keypair_deterministic` (384 seeds, cross-checked against `crypto_hash_sha512` + `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_base`), `cfg202_keypair_rng` (96, det-RNG, sk proven == next 32 RNG bytes) |\n| 203 | `cfg203_easy_open_easy` (336; mlen {0,1,31,32,33,64,1000}; per-byte tamper, wrong nonce/pk) |\n| 204 | `cfg204_detached_open_detached` (336; `m==NULL` verify-only; `_easy == mac‖c`) |\n| 205 | `cfg205_beforenm_easy_afternm` (336), `cfg205_cfg212_beforenm_construction` (108: `k == core(zero16, scalarmult(sk,pk))`) |\n| 206 | `cfg206_detached_afternm_overlap`, `cfg206_open_detached_afternm_overlap` (~1000 each: disjoint / in-place / partial-forward / partial-backward × d∈{1,7,16,31,32,63,mlen−1,mlen,mlen+1} × mlen straddling `mlen0=min(mlen,32)`), `cfg206_afternm_aliases_secretbox` |\n| 207 | `cfg207_afternm_padded` (120; 16 leading zeros out, 32 in) |\n| 208 / 209 | `cfg208_209_padded_nacl_api` (120; generic == primitive-level; padded MAC == easy MAC) |\n| 210 | `cfg210_seal_seal_open` (112), `cfg210_seal_tamper` |\n| 211 | `cfg211_xchacha_keypairs` |\n| 212 | `cfg212_xchacha_easy_detached` (126), `cfg205_cfg212_beforenm_construction` (HChacha20) |\n| 213 | `cfg213_xchacha_afternm_shapes` (144), `cfg213_xchacha_has_no_afternm_or_padded_api` |\n| 214 | `cfg214_xchacha_seal` (112) |\n| 215 | `cfg215_ecdh_agreement` (192, both primitives, + hsalsa20 ≠ hchacha20) |\n| 216 | `cfg216_constant_getters` |\n\n| ERRORS | test |\n|---|---|\n| 131, 146 | `e131_beforenm_low_order` (168: 7 blocklist encodings × bit-7 variants × 4 sks × 3 entry points) |\n| 132, 135, 136 | `e132_e135_e136_low_order_easy_detached` (70) |\n| 133, 134 | `e133_e134_easy_messagebytes_max` |\n| 137, 138 | `e137_e138_open_easy_clen_too_short` (96) |\n| 139 | `e139_open_detached_afternm_poly_mismatch` |\n| 140, 141 | `e140_e141_padded_length_floor` (192) |\n| 142, 151 | `e142_e151_seal_messagebytes_max` |\n| 143 | `e143_seal_low_order_pk_epk_still_written` (112) |\n| 144, 152 | `e144_e152_seal_open_clen_too_short` (96) |\n| 145 | `e145_seal_open_inner_failure` (72) |\n| 147, 148 | `e147_e148_xchacha_easy_messagebytes_max` |\n| 149, 150 | `e149_e150_xchacha_open_easy_clen_too_short` (96) |\n| 153 | `e153_xchacha_detached_low_order` (70) |\n\n## (c) `src/` fixes\n**None.** No C-vs-Rust divergence was found anywhere in the `crypto_box` surface. `src/aead/cryptobox.rs` is a faithful translation, including the ERRORS-143 \"epk still written on failure\" ordering (`memcpy` after the unconditional `crypto_box_easy` return capture). `src/aead/cryptobox.rs` is byte-identical to its pre-run state (verified with `cmp`).\n\n## (d) Coverage notes / corrections to the brief\n- **The `mlen > MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` branch is NOT dead.** `MESSAGEBYTES_MAX == SODIUM_SIZE_MAX − 16 == UINT64_MAX − 16`, which is *not* the largest `unsigned long long`, so `mlen = UINT64_MAX` and `UINT64_MAX − 15` do trip it: both libraries `sodium_misuse()` → `SIGABRT`. I asserted the constant's value in both libs **and** that `mlen == UINT64_MAX − 16` exactly does *not* abort in either (the call runs on into the huge `memmove` and dies on a page fault, reported as `Returned(FAULT)` by a SIGSEGV/SIGBUS marker in the forked child). This is asserted for all six misuse rows (133/134/142/147/148/151).\n- CONFIGS 213 absence check confirms `..._afternm`, `..._open_afternm`, `..._zerobytes`, `..._boxzerobytes`, `..._open` and the bare `crypto_box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305` are missing from **both** `.so`s, while the xsalsa20 primitive-level equivalents are present in both.\n- Sealed-box structure is verified constructively, not just by round-trip: the det-RNG is rewound, the ephemeral `esk` is read out of the stream via the library's own `randombytes_buf`, then `epk == scalarmult_base(esk)`, `nonce == BLAKE2b-24(epk‖pk)` and `c[32..] == crypto_box_easy(m, nonce, pk, esk)` are all asserted through both libs.\n- No row is uncovered.\n\n## Mutation checks (7 run, 6 detected, 1 provably equivalent)\n| # | mutation in `src/aead/cryptobox.rs` | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | xsalsa20 `_beforenm` uses `crypto_core_hchacha20` | **caught** — `cfg205_cfg212_beforenm_construction` |\n| M2 | `crypto_box_seal` writes `epk` only when `ret == 0` | **caught** — `e143_seal_low_order_pk_epk_still_written` |\n| M3 | `crypto_box_seal_open` guard `clen < 48` → `clen < 32` | **not caught — equivalent mutant.** For `clen ∈ [32,48)` the inner `crypto_box_open_easy` sees `clen−32 < 16` and returns −1 anyway, so the observable behaviour is identical to the C. Not a real defect. |\n| M3′ | `crypto_box_seal_open` nonce args swapped (`pk‖epk`) | **caught** — `cfg210_seal_seal_open` |\n| M4 | `crypto_box_easy_afternm` guard `>` → `>=` (off-by-one on MESSAGEBYTES_MAX) | **caught** — `e133_e134_easy_messagebytes_max` |\n| M5 | `_seed_keypair` copies `SHA-512(seed)[32..64)` | **caught** — `cfg202_seed_keypair_deterministic` |\n| M6 | `crypto_box_detached` returns 0 instead of −1 on `_beforenm` failure | **caught** — `e132_e135_e136_low_order_easy_detached` |\n| M7 | `crypto_box_easy` swaps the mac/ciphertext destinations | **caught** — `cfg204_detached_open_detached` |\n\nBuild/run command used: `cargo build && cargo test --test t08_box` (with `ulimit -c 0`; fork-heavy tests also `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, 0)` in the child). `install_det_rng` users are serialised by a file-local `Mutex` and restore `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` on the way out."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":1099892,"totalTokens":189365,"totalToolUseCount":43,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":