{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a3c139607ed1626c3","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01S9M9n7rkngWNWV4hpar9rp","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-nOS43A-translated-rust/94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051/tasks/a3c139607ed1626c3.output","summary":"`tests/t06_curve.rs` is complete: **58 tests, 0 failures, 0 warnings**, and all 9 test targets still pass with 890/890 symbol parity.\n\n## (a) Test count\n58 `#[test]` functions, 4,681 lines. Every call goes through `dlsym` on both `.so`s; every output buffer is 0xAA-prefilled with a 32-byte trailing guard and compared in full.\n\n## (b) Row → test mapping\nThe file's header comment carries the complete, verified map (checked programmatically for dangling refs). Highlights:\n- **CONFIGS 122–130** → `c122`…`c130` (+ `e180`–`e183`, `internal_pick_best_implementation`)\n- **CONFIGS 131–151** → `c131`…`c151`; ERRORS 198–221 are folded into the matching CONFIGS test where they share a gate (e.g. `c131_e198_202_…`, `c132_e203_208_…`, `c140_e210_e217_…`)\n- **CONFIGS 152–163** → `c152`…`c163`; ERRORS 162–179 in `e163`…`e179`\n- Extra: `smallorder_table_selfcheck` proves the hard-coded tables (8·P == identity, tabulated orders, 7-entry x25519 blocklist, 13 y-values) against the C before anything depends on them.\n\n## (c) `src/` fixes (2, both genuine)\n1. **`src/ed25519/h2c.rs`** — `_ => -1` → `_ => { *libc::__errno_location() = libc::EINVAL; -1 }`. C's `core_h2c_string_to_hash` `default:` sets `errno` first; Rust left it untouched. Proving rows: **ERRORS 209/218/221** (`e209_e218_e221_bad_hash_alg`); affects all 5 `_from_string*` entry points.\n2. **`src/ed25519/ge25519.rs`** — `_sodium_ge25519_frombytes_negate_vartime` wrapper wrote all 4 coordinates; C takes an early `return -1` *before* `fe25519_mul(h->T, h->X, h->Y)`, leaving `T` untouched. Now writes only X/Y/Z on the failure path. Proving symbol: SYMBOLS.md row 60 (`internal_ge25519_p3_predicates`).\n\n## (d) Rows covered with a documented caveat (all unreachable in the C, not skipped)\n- **CONFIGS 162** \"`S ≥ L` with `sig[63]&0xF0 == 0` is ACCEPTED\" is *arithmetically impossible*: `L > 2^252` ⟹ any `S ≥ L` has `sig[63] ≥ 0x10`. I drive all three reachable `&&` states and add 64 explicit proofs that a clear high nibble implies canonical. The `S+L` rejection is sharp — `(S+L)·B == S·B`, so it would verify without the guard.\n- **ERRORS 177** literal \"returns 0\" is unreachable: all 38 non-canonical encodings (y ∈ [p, p+18], both signs — enumerated exhaustively) are off-curve or off-main-subgroup. Asserted instead that non-canonical behaves *byte-identically* to its reduced canonical twin (impossible if a canonicality gate ran), plus contrast against the gated entry points.\n- **ERRORS 171 / 179 / 181 / 200 / 204 / 206** are dead branches (`MESSAGEBYTES_MAX == SIZE_MAX-64 == u64::MAX-64`; `LCOV_EXCL`; zero-output defence-in-depth; `is_on_curve` after a successful `frombytes`). Each is pinned by asserting it never fires across wide sweeps, plus the exact-gate equivalence `add(p,q) == 0 ⟺ frombytes(p) && frombytes(q)`.\n- **`crypto_core_ristretto255_from_string_nu` does not exist** in 1.0.23 (absent from the header and from both `.so`s). CONFIGS 150 is covered via the two symbols that do exist.\n- **CONFIGS 133/134** name `crypto_core_ed25519_from_uniform`/`_from_hash`, which are not public in 1.0.23; tested via the actual exports `_sodium_ge25519_from_uniform`/`_from_hash` (SYMBOLS.md rows 57/58), plus `crypto_core_ed25519_random` through the public API.\n\n## (e) Mutation checks (7 run)\n| # | Mutation | Caught by |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `sc25519::sc_is_canonical` L[31] `0x10`→`0x11` | 8 tests incl. `c141`, `c162` |\n| 2 | `has_small_order`: drop the `y·√-1 + x` term | **initially SURVIVED** → now `internal_ge25519_p3_predicates` |\n| 3 | `sign.rs`: cofactored → cofactorless final check | 9 tests incl. `c163`, `c154` |\n| 4 | drop `sodium_is_zero(n,32)` post-check in `_ed25519` | `e188_189_…` |\n| 5 | corrupt last byte of x25519 `blocklist[3]` | `e180`, `c124`, `smallorder_table_selfcheck` |\n| 6 | `scalar_add` uses 64-byte `sodium_add` (no carry drop) | `c138`, `c139`, `c149` |\n| 7 | final check keeps only the `X==0` leg (honest sigs still valid) | **`c163` alone** |\n\nMutation 2 exposed a real gap: `has_small_order`'s `fe_add` term is masked at every public call site (the main-subgroup gate rejects order-8 points independently, and the one place its result *is* the return value has its order-8 leg neutralised by `ge25519_p2_to_p3` setting `T = X·Y`). I closed it with `internal_ge25519_p3_predicates`, which drives the exported `_sodium_ge25519_{frombytes,has_small_order,is_on_curve,is_on_main_subgroup}` on a decoded `ge25519_p3` and compares the 160-byte struct as well as the verdict — that same test is what found `src/` fix #2.\n\nOne notable C behaviour now pinned by `c163`: because of the `ge25519_p2_to_p3` `T = X·Y` bug, cofactored acceptance holds unconditionally for order-2/4 torsion residuals but only when `h` is even for order-8 ones. Both regimes are asserted, and the whole construction is built through the public API (`_add` accepting a small-order operand, then an honest `S = r + h·a mod L`).","usage":{"total_tokens":332507,"tool_uses":123,"duration_ms":3440065},"uuid":"eabd14b3-8e3e-4128-953d-842abb25d31f","session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051"}
