{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_hg7tubdco6wqecl3mvl4ndzcqo2dlcgc4ll2hsm7uujkxiy4mgqa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_018ueHGV19L1ynWJGuKgAwD9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write kdf/kem/kx tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"Write ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification of libsodium 1.0.23.\n\nREPO: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nYOUR FILE (create it, nothing else): tests/t09_kdf_kem.rs\n\n## HARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify: `c_src/**` (the C is ground truth), `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`, `run_difftests.sh`, or any other `tests/t*.rs`.\n- You MAY read anything. You MAY edit files under `src/` ONLY to fix a genuine Rust translation bug proven by a failing differential test.\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly — every call goes through `dlsym` on BOTH shared libraries via the harness.\n- NEVER weaken/skip an assertion. The C is ALWAYS correct. If C and Rust disagree, the RUST is wrong.\n\n## BUILD (critical)\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib for integration tests. ALWAYS:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nulimit -c 0\ncargo build && timeout 600 cargo test --test t09_kdf_kem 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n\n## THE HARNESS (tests/common/mod.rs — READ IT FIRST)\n```rust\nmod common; use common::*;\ninit_both();\nlet (c, r) = fnpair!(\"symbol_name\", unsafe extern \"C\" fn(A,B)->R);\nassert_eq_bytes(\"what\", &c_out, &r_out);\nRng::new(SEED); pub const LENS: &[usize]; pub fn patterns(n, &mut rng) -> Vec<Vec<u8>>\nforked(|| -> i64 { ... }) -> Outcome; Outcome::Signaled(SIGABRT); assert_same_fatal(..)\ninstall_det_rng(bool); reset_det_rng();\nlibs(); sym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")\n```\n**Fork hygiene**: resolve symbols and allocate buffers in the PARENT; the child closure only calls a pre-resolved fn pointer. `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE,0)` in fork-heavy tests.\n**Parallelism**: tests are parallel threads over one pair of `.so`s; guard `install_det_rng` users with a file-local `Mutex` and restore each library's own `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` afterwards (see how `tests/t08_box.rs` does it).\n\n## WHAT TO COVER\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows **217–244** and `ERRORS.md` rows **375–391** — they are your specification.\n\n**crypto_kdf** (CONFIGS 217–225, ERRORS 375–379):\n`crypto_kdf_blake2b_derive_from_key` + `_bytes_min`/`_bytes_max`/`_contextbytes`/`_keybytes`; `crypto_kdf_derive_from_key`, `_keygen`, `_bytes_min`, `_bytes_max`, `_contextbytes`, `_keybytes`, `_primitive`; the full HKDF surface for BOTH hashes: `crypto_kdf_hkdf_sha256_{extract,extract_init,extract_update,extract_final,expand,keygen,statebytes,bytes_min,bytes_max,keybytes}` and the `sha512` equivalents.\nFacts: `crypto_kdf_BYTES_MIN=16`, `BYTES_MAX=64`, `CONTEXTBYTES=8`, `KEYBYTES=32`, primitive `\"blake2b\"`. `subkey_id` is a `uint64_t` stored little-endian into the 16-byte BLAKE2b **salt** with salt[8..16) zeroed; the 8-byte `ctx` is zero-padded into the 16-byte **personal** field. Sweep `subkey_len` ∈ {16,17,32,63,64} × `subkey_id` ∈ {0,1,0xFF,2^56,u64::MAX} and reject 0,15,65 (-1 + errno EINVAL).\nHKDF: sha256 `KEYBYTES=32`, `BYTES_MAX=0xff*32=8160`; sha512 `KEYBYTES=64`, `BYTES_MAX=0xff*64=16320`. `expand` with `out_len > BYTES_MAX` → -1 + EINVAL. Sweep `out_len` ∈ {0,1,31,32,33,64,8160} (sha256) / {0,1,63,64,65,128,16320} (sha512) — 8160/16320 make the single-byte counter reach 255. Streaming `extract_init`/`extract_update`×N/`extract_final` MUST equal the one-shot `extract`. `salt_len` 0 (salt may be NULL) vs >0 vs > block size; `ikm_len` 0 vs 1 vs > block size.\n\n**crypto_kem** (CONFIGS 226–238, ERRORS 380–387):\n`crypto_kem_mlkem768_{keypair,seed_keypair,enc,enc_deterministic,dec,publickeybytes,secretkeybytes,ciphertextbytes,sharedsecretbytes,seedbytes}`; `crypto_kem_xwing_{keypair,seed_keypair,enc,enc_deterministic,dec,...}`; `crypto_kem_{keypair,seed_keypair,enc,dec}` + getters + `_primitive`.\nSizes: mlkem768 pk **1184**, sk **2400**, ct **1088**, ss **32**, seed **64**. xwing pk **1216**, sk **32**, ct **1120**, ss **32**, seed **32**; `crypto_kem_PRIMITIVE == \"xwing\"`.\nThe `*_seed_keypair` and `*_enc_deterministic` entry points are FULLY deterministic — these are your primary byte-for-byte comparison points and need no RNG shim. Sweep ≥64 random seeds each.\nERRORS 380/381/383/384/385/386: build a NON-CANONICAL mlkem768 pk (a 12-bit coefficient ≥ `MLKEM768_Q`=3329 in the first 1152 bytes; the trailing 32-byte seed is never validated) → `_enc` returns -1. And a LOW-ORDER X25519 pk/ct component for xwing (get the 7 blocklisted encodings from `has_small_order`'s table in `c_src/libsodium/crypto_scalarmult/curve25519/ref10/x25519_ref10.c`) → -1.\nERRORS 382: `crypto_kem_mlkem768_dec` NEVER returns non-zero — a tampered ct is handled by constant-time implicit rejection (`cmov` of `SHAKE256(z‖ct)`). Tamper at many byte positions: assert BOTH libs return 0 AND produce the SAME (wrong) shared secret. Also assert `ss_enc == ss_dec` for untampered ct.\nCONFIGS 232: sweep MANY seeds so `rej_uniform`'s `while (ctr < 256)` refill arm fires (seed-dependent), covering both `transposed=0` (keypair) and `transposed=1` (enc).\n\n**crypto_kx** (CONFIGS 239–244, ERRORS 388–391):\n`crypto_kx_{keypair,seed_keypair,client_session_keys,server_session_keys,publickeybytes,secretkeybytes,seedbytes,sessionkeybytes,primitive}`. All sizes 32; primitive `\"x25519blake2b\"`.\n`_seed_keypair`: `sk = BLAKE2b-32(seed)` then X25519 base — deterministic.\nFull handshake property: `client.tx == server.rx` and `client.rx == server.tx`. Both hash BLAKE2b with outlen 64 over (`q ‖ client_pk ‖ server_pk`); client takes rx=keys[0..32), tx=keys[32..64); server the reverse.\nERRORS 388/390: `rx == NULL && tx == NULL` → `sodium_misuse()` → SIGABRT in BOTH (use `forked`). CONFIGS 242/243: `rx == NULL` alone (tx-only) and `tx == NULL` alone (rx-only) are VALID shapes where the args are aliased — assert the single output equals the corresponding half.\nERRORS 389/391: a low-order peer pk → -1.\n\nC sources: `c_src/libsodium/crypto_kdf/**`, `crypto_kem/**`, `crypto_kx/**`. Headers in `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/`. READ them for exact signatures.\n\n### Requirements\n1. **Every** CONFIGS row 217–244 gets a `#[test]` (or clearly-named section) driving ≥64 randomized inputs where a random shape exists; assert the iteration count in-test.\n2. **Every** ERRORS row 375–391 gets a C-vs-Rust assertion on the return value AND the full output buffer AND errno where applicable.\n3. Prefill every output buffer with 0xAA plus a trailing guard; compare the FULL buffer and assert the guard is intact.\n4. Opaque HKDF states: allocate via `crypto_kdf_hkdf_sha256_statebytes()` / `_sha512_statebytes()`, oversized and 0xAA-prefilled, and compare the WHOLE state buffer between libs after `_extract_init` and each `_extract_update`.\n5. Round-trips: kem enc→dec (ss match), kx client↔server, kdf hkdf extract→expand.\n\n## ON DIVERGENCE\n1. Print exact inputs and the first differing byte offset.\n2. Rust sources: `src/kdf_kem/{kdf,kem,kx,mlkem,xof,softaes}.rs`, `src/primitives/blake2b.rs`, `src/ed25519/x25519.rs`.\n3. Fix the Rust to match the C EXACTLY. For mlkem the usual culprits are NTT/montgomery reduction constants, the 12-bit polyvec packing, and the `du`/`dv` compression rounding.\n4. Re-run and confirm.\n5. Report EVERY `src/` edit with a one-line before/after and the proving row.\n\n## DELIVERABLE\n`tests/t09_kdf_kem.rs` compiling and PASSING. Report: (a) test count, (b) CONFIGS/ERRORS rows → test fn mapping, (c) every `src/` fix with the proving row, (d) any row not covered and precisely why. Also run ≥5 mutation checks (break a Rust fn, confirm a test catches it, revert; verify the file is byte-identical afterwards) and report them."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2253,"cache_read_input_tokens":259134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"963d05f9-0586-49b5-9373-1a1917a43966","timestamp":"2026-08-14T18:37:35.319Z"}
