{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_hg7tubdco6wqecl3mvl4ndzcqo2dlcgc4ll2hsm7uujkxiy4mgqa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01QTgmPYPBc1PevzSJ5kHasn","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write pwhash/scrypt tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"Write ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification of libsodium 1.0.23.\n\nREPO: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nYOUR FILE (create it, nothing else): tests/t10_pwhash.rs\n\n## HARD RULES\n- Do NOT modify: `c_src/**` (the C is ground truth), `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`, `run_difftests.sh`, or any other `tests/t*.rs`.\n- You MAY read anything. You MAY edit files under `src/` ONLY to fix a genuine Rust translation bug proven by a failing differential test.\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly — every call goes through `dlsym` on BOTH shared libraries via the harness.\n- NEVER weaken/skip an assertion. The C is ALWAYS correct. If C and Rust disagree, the RUST is wrong.\n\n## BUILD (critical)\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib for integration tests. ALWAYS:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-nOS43A/translated_rust\nulimit -c 0\ncargo build && timeout 600 cargo test --test t10_pwhash 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n**PERFORMANCE MATTERS HERE.** Argon2/scrypt are deliberately slow, and the Rust cdylib is built unoptimised in the debug profile. Keep `opslimit`/`memlimit` at their MINIMUM values for almost every case (argon2i: ops 3, mem 8192; argon2id: ops 1, mem 8192; scrypt: ops 32768, mem 16777216). Budget the whole test target under ~300 s; if a single case is slow, reduce the number of randomized inputs for THAT case (and say so in your report) rather than dropping the row. Use `#[test]` granularity so cargo can run them in parallel.\n\n## THE HARNESS (tests/common/mod.rs — READ IT FIRST)\n```rust\nmod common; use common::*;\ninit_both();\nlet (c, r) = fnpair!(\"symbol_name\", unsafe extern \"C\" fn(A,B)->R);\nassert_eq_bytes(\"what\", &c_out, &r_out);\nRng::new(SEED); pub fn patterns(n, &mut rng) -> Vec<Vec<u8>>\nforked(|| -> i64 { ... }) -> Outcome; Outcome::Signaled(SIGABRT); assert_same_fatal(..)\ninstall_det_rng(bool); reset_det_rng();\nlibs(); sym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")\n```\n**Fork hygiene**: resolve symbols and allocate buffers in the PARENT; the child closure only calls a pre-resolved fn pointer. `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE,0)` in fork-heavy tests.\n**Parallelism**: guard `install_det_rng` users with a file-local `Mutex` and restore each library's `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` afterwards (see `tests/t08_box.rs`).\n**`_str` functions consume randomness** (random salt), so they are NOT byte-comparable across libs unless you `install_det_rng` + `reset_det_rng()` before each paired call. Do that — then the produced strings must be IDENTICAL. Also test cross-verification: a string produced by C must verify under Rust and vice versa.\n\n## WHAT TO COVER\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows **245–264** and `ERRORS.md` rows **258–374** (that is 117 error rows — the largest block in the project) — they are your specification.\n\n**Public argon2 API** (CONFIGS 245–255, ERRORS 316–347):\n`crypto_pwhash`, `_str`, `_str_alg`, `_str_verify`, `_str_needs_rehash`, and every constant getter (`_alg_argon2i13`=1, `_alg_argon2id13`=2, `_alg_default`=2, `_bytes_min`=16, `_bytes_max`, `_passwd_min`, `_passwd_max`, `_saltbytes`=16, `_strbytes`=128, `_strprefix`, `_opslimit_min/_interactive/_moderate/_sensitive/_max`, `_memlimit_*`, `_primitive`); the same for `crypto_pwhash_argon2i_*` and `crypto_pwhash_argon2id_*`.\nNote the DIFFERENT minima: argon2i `OPSLIMIT_MIN=3`, argon2id `OPSLIMIT_MIN=1`; both `MEMLIMIT_MIN=8192`, `BYTES_MIN=16`, `BYTES_MAX=4294967295`, `MEMLIMIT_MAX=4398046510080`.\nCONFIGS 249: `outlen` 65 and 128 exercise `blake2b_long`'s CHUNKED mode (`outlen > 64`) — do not skip, it is a distinct code path.\nERRORS 345: `crypto_pwhash_str_alg` with `alg` ∉ {1,2} → `sodium_misuse()` → SIGABRT (NOT -1). ERRORS 344: `crypto_pwhash` with `alg` ∉ {1,2} → -1 + EINVAL. Test alg ∈ {0,3,-1,255,0x7fffffff} — out-of-range enums crossing FFI.\nERRORS 323/331: `(const void*)out == (const void*)passwd` aliasing → -1 + EINVAL.\nERRORS 343/359: `_str_needs_rehash` returns **1** (not an error) when the encoded params differ from the requested ones.\n\n**argon2 internals reachable via the public API** (ERRORS 258–315):\nMost `argon2_validate_inputs` / `argon2_decode_string` / `argon2_encode_string` branches are NOT directly exported. Reach them through:\n- `crypto_pwhash_str_verify` / `crypto_pwhash_argon2i_str_verify` / `crypto_pwhash_argon2id_str_verify` fed HAND-CRAFTED encoded strings. This is how you cover essentially all of ERRORS 290–309: wrong prefix, missing `$v=`, `$v=16` (→ ARGON2_INCORRECT_TYPE, surfaces as -1), missing/`,`-less `m=`/`t=`/`p=` fields, non-minimal decimals like `m=065536`, overflowing decimals, bad base64 salt/hash, decoded `saltlen<8`, decoded `outlen<16`, `m_cost<8`, `m_cost<8*p`, `t=0`, `p=0`, `p>16777215`, trailing garbage, and a non-NUL-terminated `str`.\n- `crypto_pwhash_str_needs_rehash` for ERRORS 339–343.\n- First check with `nm -D` whether `argon2_hash`, `argon2_verify`, `argon2i_hash_raw`, `argon2id_hash_raw`, `argon2_ctx`, `argon2_encode_string`, `argon2_decode_string`, `argon2_error_message` are EXPORTED by the C `.so`. If they are, drive them DIRECTLY — that is far better coverage and lets you assert the exact `ARGON2_*` numeric codes (values are in `c_src/libsodium/crypto_pwhash/argon2/argon2.h`). Report which ones you found.\n- For rows whose branch is genuinely unreachable through any exported symbol, say so explicitly and assert the closest reachable behaviour.\n\n**scrypt** (CONFIGS 256–264, ERRORS 348–374):\n`crypto_pwhash_scryptsalsa208sha256`, `_str`, `_str_verify`, `_str_needs_rehash`, `_ll`, and every constant getter (`_bytes_min`=16, `_bytes_max`, `_passwd_min`, `_passwd_max`, `_saltbytes`=32, `_strbytes`=102, `_strprefix`=`\"$7$\"`, `_opslimit_min`=32768, `_opslimit_max`, `_memlimit_min`=16777216, `_memlimit_max`, `_opslimit_interactive`, `_memlimit_interactive`, `_opslimit_sensitive`, `_memlimit_sensitive` — note there is NO MODERATE preset).\n`crypto_pwhash_scryptsalsa208sha256_ll` is the LOWEST-LEVEL entry point and must be driven directly (CONFIGS 256/257): `N` ∈ {2,4,16,1024} (power of two, ≥2) × `r` ∈ {1,8} × `p` ∈ {1,2,3,4} × `buflen` ∈ {32,33,64,80} × `saltlen` ∈ {0,32}. `p>1` exercises the multi-block `smix` loop. Invalid: N=0,1,3,1000 (→ -1 EINVAL), r=0, p=0, `r*p >= 2^30` (→ -1 EFBIG), `N > UINT32_MAX` (→ -1 EFBIG).\nCONFIGS 260 / ERRORS: `opslimit` below `OPSLIMIT_MIN` is silently CLAMPED UP to 32768, NOT rejected — assert both libs produce the SAME hash as `opslimit=32768`.\nCONFIGS 258/259: `pickparams` branch A (`opslimit < memlimit/32` ⇒ p=1) vs branch B (⇒ p may be >1). Pick concrete (opslimit, memlimit) pairs that land in each.\nERRORS 354: `_str_verify` requires `sodium_strnlen(str,102) == 101` exactly.\n\nC sources: `c_src/libsodium/crypto_pwhash/**`. Headers in `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/crypto_pwhash*.h`. READ them for exact signatures.\n\n### Requirements\n1. **Every** CONFIGS row 245–264 gets a `#[test]` (or clearly-named section). Use ≥64 randomized inputs where affordable; where a single case costs >50 ms, use fewer and SAY SO in your report.\n2. **Every** ERRORS row 258–374 gets a C-vs-Rust assertion on the return value AND the full output buffer AND errno where applicable. Where a row's exact numeric `ARGON2_*` code is observable (direct `argon2_*` export), assert the NUMBER, not just \"failed\".\n3. Prefill every output buffer with 0xAA plus a trailing guard; compare the FULL buffer and assert the guard is intact.\n4. Cross-verification: strings produced by C verify under Rust and vice versa, for argon2i, argon2id and scrypt.\n5. Group the 117 error rows into a manageable number of table-driven tests — a `&[(input, expected_ret, expected_errno)]` table with the ERRORS row number as a label is ideal, and makes the coverage auditable.\n\n## ON DIVERGENCE\n1. Print exact inputs (alg, opslimit, memlimit, outlen, passwd, salt, the encoded string) and the first differing byte offset.\n2. Rust sources: `src/pwhash/{argon2,crypto_pwhash,scrypt}.rs`.\n3. Fix the Rust to match the C EXACTLY. Likely culprits: the `m_cost` rounding to a multiple of `4*lanes`, the argon2id data-dependent/independent switch (`pass==0 && slice<2`), `blake2b_long`'s chunked mode, `decode_decimal`'s non-minimal/overflow rejection, `pickparams`' clamping, and the itoa64 alphabet.\n4. Re-run and confirm.\n5. Report EVERY `src/` edit with a one-line before/after and the proving row.\n\n## DELIVERABLE\n`tests/t10_pwhash.rs` compiling and PASSING within the time budget. Report: (a) test count and wall-clock runtime, (b) CONFIGS/ERRORS rows → test fn mapping (all 117 error rows must be accounted for, even if the account is \"unreachable via any exported symbol because X\"), (c) every `src/` fix with the proving row, (d) which `argon2_*` internals are exported by the C `.so`. Also run ≥5 mutation checks and report them."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2253,"cache_read_input_tokens":259134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94902601-9803-4a17-b8b6-a77154607051","uuid":"0dcd8b88-6ddb-4790-98c2-8fccf01b0c6e","timestamp":"2026-08-14T18:38:20.522Z"}
